{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016hinboNb6VGZstL46Nxxo4","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 section 11.4.7 site-specific ground motion procedures 21.1 21.2 21.3 21.4\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21\"},{\"title\":\"Design Ground Motions | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/design-ground-motions\"},{\"title\":\"Site Ground Motions | UpCodes\",\"url\":\"https://up.codes/s/site-ground-motions\"},{\"title\":\"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria for Buildings and Other Structures - DOKUMEN.PUB\",\"url\":\"https://dokumen.pub/asce-7-22-minimum-design-loads-and-associated-criteria-for-buildings-and-other-structures.html\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1\"},{\"title\":\"Minimum Design Loads for Buildings and Other Structures\\b 385 CHAPTER C11\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784412916.chc11c22\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about ASCE 7-22 and site-specific ground motion procedures. Here's what I found:\n\nASCE/SEI 7-22 includes Chapter 21 on Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design, and Section 11.4.7 addresses Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures.\n\nThe search results also indicate that a web tool calculates risk-targeted ground motion values from probabilistic seismic hazard curves in accordance with the site-specific ground motion procedures defined in \"Method 2\" (Section 21.2.1.2) of the ASCE/SEI 7-10, 7-16 and 7-22 standards.\n\nAdditionally, exceptions exist where a site-specific ground motion analysis is performed in accordance with Section 11.4.7.\n\nRegarding the specific subsections you mentioned (21.1, 21.2, 21.3, 21.4), the search results reference these sections in relation to site-specific procedures, but don't provide detailed breakdowns of each individual subsection. For comprehensive details on the specific requirements within each of these sections, you would need to access the full ASCE/SEI 7-22 standard directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"3355873e-38f8-498d-96bc-7e41fde0bfa3","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:41:51.572Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 section 11.4.7 site-specific ground motion procedures 21.1 21.2 21.3 21.4","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GVvSj1svTuh7nfC9Bb8qb1","content":[{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21"},{"title":"Design Ground Motions | U.S. Geological Survey","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/design-ground-motions"},{"title":"Site Ground Motions | UpCodes","url":"https://up.codes/s/site-ground-motions"},{"title":"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria for Buildings and Other Structures - DOKUMEN.PUB","url":"https://dokumen.pub/asce-7-22-minimum-design-loads-and-associated-criteria-for-buildings-and-other-structures.html"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1"},{"title":"Minimum Design Loads for Buildings and Other Structures\b 385 CHAPTER C11","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784412916.chc11c22"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about ASCE 7-22 and site-specific ground motion procedures. Here's what I found:\n\nASCE/SEI 7-22 includes Chapter 21 on Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design, and Section 11.4.7 addresses Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures.\n\nThe search results also indicate that a web tool calculates risk-targeted ground motion values from probabilistic seismic hazard curves in accordance with the site-specific ground motion procedures defined in \"Method 2\" (Section 21.2.1.2) of the ASCE/SEI 7-10, 7-16 and 7-22 standards.\n\nAdditionally, exceptions exist where a site-specific ground motion analysis is performed in accordance with Section 11.4.7.\n\nRegarding the specific subsections you mentioned (21.1, 21.2, 21.3, 21.4), the search results reference these sections in relation to site-specific procedures, but don't provide detailed breakdowns of each individual subsection. For comprehensive details on the specific requirements within each of these sections, you would need to access the full ASCE/SEI 7-22 standard directly."],"durationSeconds":5.30564673}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XDvuKkvg3kXMT72dTVgKW1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 scale factors maximum horizontal geometric mean 1.2 0.2s 1.25 1.0s 1.3 10s seismic\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"www.cedengineering.com Seismic Design of Structures According to ASCE/SEI 7-22\",\"url\":\"https://www.cedengineering.com/userfiles/S03-028%20-%20Seismic%20Design%20of%20Structures%20According%20to%20ASCE%20SEI%207-22.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11\"},{\"title\":\"Asce 7-22 Ch 22com_for Pc\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512407802/Asce-7-22-Ch-22com-for-Pc\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 | ASCE\",\"url\":\"https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/codes-and-standards/asce-sei-7-22\"},{\"title\":\"Frequently Asked Seismic Questions\",\"url\":\"https://www.structuremag.org/article/frequently-asked-seismic-questions/\"},{\"title\":\"Calculation Modules > Loads & Forces Divisions > ASCE 7-22 Seismic Base Shear\",\"url\":\"https://media.enercalc.com/sel_help_20/asceseismic_2.htm\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about ASCE 7-22 scale factors for horizontal geometric mean with the specific values you mentioned (1.2 at 0.2s, 1.25 at 1.0s, and 1.3 at 10s).\n\nThe search results provide general information about ASCE 7-22 seismic design provisions, including references to changed maximum-response scale factors and information about the use of geometric mean response spectral acceleration. However, the specific scale factor values and thresholds you mentioned (1.2, 1.25, 1.3 at those particular periods) are not explicitly detailed in these search results.\n\nThe results do indicate that ASCE 7-22 introduces a multi-period response spectrum available via a web-based tool to provide acceleration parameters, which suggests these scale factors may be part of that multi-period spectrum framework rather than traditional two-period (0.2s and 1.0s) values.\n\nFor more detailed information about these specific scale factor values and their application in ASCE 7-22, you may need to consult the full ASCE 7-22 standard directly or access the USGS ASCE 7-22 design database that's referenced in the standard.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"d6896fc7-9ba6-4118-9c56-c2e3935642b1","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:41:52.828Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 scale factors maximum horizontal geometric mean 1.2 0.2s 1.25 1.0s 1.3 10s seismic","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_019jfq7cEcBXDBpcCFZq2qmP","content":[{"title":"www.cedengineering.com Seismic Design of Structures According to ASCE/SEI 7-22","url":"https://www.cedengineering.com/userfiles/S03-028%20-%20Seismic%20Design%20of%20Structures%20According%20to%20ASCE%20SEI%207-22.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11"},{"title":"Asce 7-22 Ch 22com_for Pc","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512407802/Asce-7-22-Ch-22com-for-Pc"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou"},{"title":"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation","url":"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 | ASCE","url":"https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/codes-and-standards/asce-sei-7-22"},{"title":"Frequently Asked Seismic Questions","url":"https://www.structuremag.org/article/frequently-asked-seismic-questions/"},{"title":"Calculation Modules > Loads & Forces Divisions > ASCE 7-22 Seismic Base Shear","url":"https://media.enercalc.com/sel_help_20/asceseismic_2.htm"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query about ASCE 7-22 scale factors for horizontal geometric mean with the specific values you mentioned (1.2 at 0.2s, 1.25 at 1.0s, and 1.3 at 10s).\n\nThe search results provide general information about ASCE 7-22 seismic design provisions, including references to changed maximum-response scale factors and information about the use of geometric mean response spectral acceleration. However, the specific scale factor values and thresholds you mentioned (1.2, 1.25, 1.3 at those particular periods) are not explicitly detailed in these search results.\n\nThe results do indicate that ASCE 7-22 introduces a multi-period response spectrum available via a web-based tool to provide acceleration parameters, which suggests these scale factors may be part of that multi-period spectrum framework rather than traditional two-period (0.2s and 1.0s) values.\n\nFor more detailed information about these specific scale factor values and their application in ASCE 7-22, you may need to consult the full ASCE 7-22 standard directly or access the USGS ASCE 7-22 design database that's referenced in the standard."],"durationSeconds":5.0763115629999955}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FvNgqVFCMaFDyXmMYGjaxi","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 section 21.4 design acceleration parameters SDS SD1 site-specific formula\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Design Example 3 Site-specific Ground Motion Procedures ...\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S181-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Load Calculation as per ASCE 7-16\",\"url\":\"https://rcenggstudios.com/seismic-load-calculation-as-per-asce-7-16/\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Design Categories A–F — IBC / ASCE 7 SDC Tables\",\"url\":\"https://steelcalculator.app/reference/seismic-design-categories/\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11\"},{\"title\":\"Page 1 of 18 CHANGES IN SEISMIC DESIGN CRITERIA ...\",\"url\":\"https://seau.starchapter.com/images/downloads/SEAUNews_Documents/asce7_16workshop_whitepaper_20200131_final.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's information about ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4 and design acceleration parameters SDS and SD1:\n\n## Site-Specific Design Procedures\n\nAccording to Section 21.4, for use with the equivalent lateral force procedure, the site-specific acceleration, Sa, at T shall be permitted to replace SD1/T in Equation 12.8-3 and SD1TL/T² in Equation 12.8-4.\n\n## Design Spectral Acceleration Parameters\n\nTwo additional parameters SDS and SD1 are used to define design spectral accelerations, with values of SDS and SD1 (2/3 SMS and 2/3 SM1) provided online by the USGS Seismic Design Web Service, for user-specified site location and site class.\n\n## Site-Specific Ground Motion Analysis\n\nThis procedure illustrates the general method for deriving the site-specific design response spectrum based on a given site-specific response spectrum analysis per Sections 21.1 and 21.2.\n\n## Design Spectrum Development\n\nSection 21.4 addresses determining values of SDS and SD1 from a site-specific design. The design parameters incorporate site-specific ground motions developed through either site response analysis or probabilistic ground motion hazard analysis as outlined in Chapter 21 of ASCE 7-22.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"81973f3a-a046-45fd-b46e-c410dd3a204b","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:41:53.222Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 section 21.4 design acceleration parameters SDS SD1 site-specific formula","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012T5GRDUywA7AvvLSxPshMu","content":[{"title":"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"Design Example 3 Site-specific Ground Motion Procedures ...","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S181-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"Seismic Load Calculation as per ASCE 7-16","url":"https://rcenggstudios.com/seismic-load-calculation-as-per-asce-7-16/"},{"title":"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation","url":"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou"},{"title":"Seismic Design Categories A–F — IBC / ASCE 7 SDC Tables","url":"https://steelcalculator.app/reference/seismic-design-categories/"},{"title":"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 11 - Seismic Design Criteria | ASCE Amplify","url":"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11"},{"title":"Page 1 of 18 CHANGES IN SEISMIC DESIGN CRITERIA ...","url":"https://seau.starchapter.com/images/downloads/SEAUNews_Documents/asce7_16workshop_whitepaper_20200131_final.pdf"}]},"Based on the web search results, here's information about ASCE 7-22 Section 21.4 and design acceleration parameters SDS and SD1:\n\n## Site-Specific Design Procedures\n\nAccording to Section 21.4, for use with the equivalent lateral force procedure, the site-specific acceleration, Sa, at T shall be permitted to replace SD1/T in Equation 12.8-3 and SD1TL/T² in Equation 12.8-4.\n\n## Design Spectral Acceleration Parameters\n\nTwo additional parameters SDS and SD1 are used to define design spectral accelerations, with values of SDS and SD1 (2/3 SMS and 2/3 SM1) provided online by the USGS Seismic Design Web Service, for user-specified site location and site class.\n\n## Site-Specific Ground Motion Analysis\n\nThis procedure illustrates the general method for deriving the site-specific design response spectrum based on a given site-specific response spectrum analysis per Sections 21.1 and 21.2.\n\n## Design Spectrum Development\n\nSection 21.4 addresses determining values of SDS and SD1 from a site-specific design. The design parameters incorporate site-specific ground motions developed through either site response analysis or probabilistic ground motion hazard analysis as outlined in Chapter 21 of ASCE 7-22."],"durationSeconds":6.2877938030000005}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pp2j47rDuR7daDytDRwxiF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MCER probabilistic 1 percent probability collapse 50 years ASCE 7\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Seismic Response History Procedures for the ASCE/SEI 7- ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Marshall-Lew/publication/262335586_Seismic_Response_History_Procedures_for_the_ASCESEI_7-10_Standard/links/5492f87d0cf2213b86cbb5ac/Seismic-Response-History-Procedures-for-the-ASCE-SEI-7-10-Standard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Return Periods Seismology Group, AECOM Los Angeles\",\"url\":\"https://www.jeff-bayless.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ReturnPeriods-4April2019.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Use of MCER Spectrum in Lieu of Design Response Spectrum | Eng-Tips\",\"url\":\"https://www.eng-tips.com/threads/use-of-mcer-spectrum-in-lieu-of-design-response-spectrum.488460/\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-05 MCE vs. ASCE 7-10 MCEr | Eng-Tips\",\"url\":\"https://www.eng-tips.com/threads/asce-7-05-mce-vs-asce-7-10-mcer.384605/\"},{\"title\":\"Ground Motion Levels per ASCE 7-16 with ideCAD\",\"url\":\"https://help.idecad.com/ideCAD/earthquake-ground-motion-levels-1\"},{\"title\":\"Probability of collapse in 50 years. | Download Table\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Probability-of-collapse-in-50-years_tbl3_305508501\"},{\"title\":\"ISSN 1520-295X Seismic Performance Assessment of\",\"url\":\"https://www.eng.buffalo.edu/mceer-reports/18/18-0004.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Collapse-proof seismic design | ASCE\",\"url\":\"https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/civil-engineering-source/civil-engineering-magazine/issues/magazine-issue/article/2024/09/collapse-proof-seismic-design\"},{\"title\":\"How should I calculate the seismic design loads for a short term service year i.e., 10 years ? | ResearchGate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/post/How-should-I-calculate-the-seismic-design-loads-for-a-short-term-service-year-ie-10-years\"},{\"title\":\"Risk-Informed Mean Recurrence Intervals for Updated Wind Maps in ASCE 7-16 - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5988364/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here is information about MCER, probabilistic ground motions, and the 1 percent probability of collapse in 50 years in ASCE 7:\n\n## MCER Definition and Risk-Targeting\n\nThe probabilistic MCE ground motions in ASCE 7-10 are defined as having a 2 percent probability of being exceeded in 50 years, and these are modified with geographical adjustments so that a uniform collapse probability of 1 percent in 50 years is achieved across the United States.\n\nThis process determines the probabilistic ground motion response spectrum ordinates for each spectral period by iterative integration of site-specific hazard curves with a lognormal probability density function representing the collapse fragility to achieve a 1 percent probability of collapse within a 50-year period.\n\n## Shift from Uniform Hazard to Uniform Risk\n\nA key distinction is that previous editions provided a uniform seismic hazard such that earthquakes had a 2% probability of exceedance in 50 years, while the new maps provide a uniform risk of collapse for a selected collapse probability.\n\n## ASCE 7 Performance Objective\n\nThe reliability-based seismic performance goal for Risk Category II buildings is a 1% (or less) probability of collapse in 50 years.\n\nThe search results indicate that this 1 percent collapse probability in 50 years is the core risk target that ASCE 7-10 and later editions use to develop the MCER ground motion maps.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"0c28fb29-b112-4462-acc6-236b428cd9fe","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:41:54.717Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"MCER probabilistic 1 percent probability collapse 50 years ASCE 7","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01JkSAZrVyiH6MWXVRHFwY76","content":[{"title":"Seismic Response History Procedures for the ASCE/SEI 7- ...","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Marshall-Lew/publication/262335586_Seismic_Response_History_Procedures_for_the_ASCESEI_7-10_Standard/links/5492f87d0cf2213b86cbb5ac/Seismic-Response-History-Procedures-for-the-ASCE-SEI-7-10-Standard.pdf"},{"title":"Introduction to Return Periods Seismology Group, AECOM Los Angeles","url":"https://www.jeff-bayless.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ReturnPeriods-4April2019.pdf"},{"title":"Use of MCER Spectrum in Lieu of Design Response Spectrum | Eng-Tips","url":"https://www.eng-tips.com/threads/use-of-mcer-spectrum-in-lieu-of-design-response-spectrum.488460/"},{"title":"ASCE 7-05 MCE vs. ASCE 7-10 MCEr | Eng-Tips","url":"https://www.eng-tips.com/threads/asce-7-05-mce-vs-asce-7-10-mcer.384605/"},{"title":"Ground Motion Levels per ASCE 7-16 with ideCAD","url":"https://help.idecad.com/ideCAD/earthquake-ground-motion-levels-1"},{"title":"Probability of collapse in 50 years. | Download Table","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Probability-of-collapse-in-50-years_tbl3_305508501"},{"title":"ISSN 1520-295X Seismic Performance Assessment of","url":"https://www.eng.buffalo.edu/mceer-reports/18/18-0004.pdf"},{"title":"Collapse-proof seismic design | ASCE","url":"https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/civil-engineering-source/civil-engineering-magazine/issues/magazine-issue/article/2024/09/collapse-proof-seismic-design"},{"title":"How should I calculate the seismic design loads for a short term service year i.e., 10 years ? | ResearchGate","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/post/How-should-I-calculate-the-seismic-design-loads-for-a-short-term-service-year-ie-10-years"},{"title":"Risk-Informed Mean Recurrence Intervals for Updated Wind Maps in ASCE 7-16 - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5988364/"}]},"Based on the search results, here is information about MCER, probabilistic ground motions, and the 1 percent probability of collapse in 50 years in ASCE 7:\n\n## MCER Definition and Risk-Targeting\n\nThe probabilistic MCE ground motions in ASCE 7-10 are defined as having a 2 percent probability of being exceeded in 50 years, and these are modified with geographical adjustments so that a uniform collapse probability of 1 percent in 50 years is achieved across the United States.\n\nThis process determines the probabilistic ground motion response spectrum ordinates for each spectral period by iterative integration of site-specific hazard curves with a lognormal probability density function representing the collapse fragility to achieve a 1 percent probability of collapse within a 50-year period.\n\n## Shift from Uniform Hazard to Uniform Risk\n\nA key distinction is that previous editions provided a uniform seismic hazard such that earthquakes had a 2% probability of exceedance in 50 years, while the new maps provide a uniform risk of collapse for a selected collapse probability.\n\n## ASCE 7 Performance Objective\n\nThe reliability-based seismic performance goal for Risk Category II buildings is a 1% (or less) probability of collapse in 50 years.\n\nThe search results indicate that this 1 percent collapse probability in 50 years is the core risk target that ASCE 7-10 and later editions use to develop the MCER ground motion maps."],"durationSeconds":6.123778880999998}}
