{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Uh2wHStgjmEcx5EDjKoiTk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7-22 SMS SD1 relationship \"1.5\" \"SDS\" \"SD1\" back-calculation site-specific\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Design Parameters\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/of/2001/ofr-01-437/ofr-01-437.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Load Calculation as per ASCE 7-16\",\"url\":\"https://rcenggstudios.com/seismic-load-calculation-as-per-asce-7-16/\"},{\"title\":\"1535 EDGEWATER ST. NW, SALEM, OR 97304 503-378-4133 OREGON.GOV/BCD\",\"url\":\"https://www.oregon.gov/bcd/codes-stand/Documents/sam-24-03-multi-period-response.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CE 160 Vukazich Lateral Load Path IBC Static Seismic Forces [L9 L10] 1/28\",\"url\":\"https://www.sjsu.edu/people/steven.vukazich/docs/IBC%20EQ%20notes%20S17%20Labs%209%20and%2010.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou\"},{\"title\":\"New Site-Specific Ground Motion Requirements of ASCE 7-16\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.ymaws.com/seaoc.site-ym.com/resource/resmgr/Convention_Proceedings/2017/56R1_PAPER_Kircher_R1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SEAW Earthquake Engineering Committee Voluntary Use of Multi-Period Response\",\"url\":\"https://sunfish-daffodil-ebml.squarespace.com/s/White-Paper-EEC-4-2022.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding the SMS/SD1 and SDS/SD1 relationships in ASCE 7-22:\n\n## Key Relationships\n\nSMS = 1.5 x SDS and SM1 = 1.5 x SD1, representing the Risk-Targeted Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCER) spectral response acceleration parameters.\n\nConversely, design spectral accelerations (SDS and SD1) are obtained by multiplying SMS and SM1 by 2/3 - that is SDS = (2/3) SMS and SD1 = (2/3) SM1.\n\n## Back-Calculation and Site-Specific Applications\n\nThe relationship can be inverted for back-calculation purposes. If you know SDS and SD1 values from a site-specific analysis, you can derive the corresponding SMS and SM1 values by multiplying by 1.5. Conversely, if you have SMS and SM1 values, divide by 1.5 (or multiply by 2/3) to obtain SDS and SD1.\n\n## Site-Specific Context\n\nStructural design in ASCE 7 is performed for earthquake demands that are 2/3 of the MCER response spectra, and these parameters define a design response spectrum for sites of any characteristics. For site-specific ground motion analyses, site-specific requirements recognize that periods of peak response are not always at 0.2 s and 1.0 s and require that SD1 be taken as equal to 2 times the response spectral acceleration at 2 s with greater than 1-s response spectral acceleration.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"b73c1f5f-f6bc-4d90-89b7-b383741873eb","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:10.306Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7-22 SMS SD1 relationship \"1.5\" \"SDS\" \"SD1\" back-calculation site-specific","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01YNqDGewRH1o3NqbVVKj1wk","content":[{"title":"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE7-22 Web Service Documentation","url":"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html"},{"title":"Seismic Design Parameters","url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/of/2001/ofr-01-437/ofr-01-437.pdf"},{"title":"Seismic Load Calculation as per ASCE 7-16","url":"https://rcenggstudios.com/seismic-load-calculation-as-per-asce-7-16/"},{"title":"1535 EDGEWATER ST. NW, SALEM, OR 97304 503-378-4133 OREGON.GOV/BCD","url":"https://www.oregon.gov/bcd/codes-stand/Documents/sam-24-03-multi-period-response.pdf"},{"title":"CE 160 Vukazich Lateral Load Path IBC Static Seismic Forces [L9 L10] 1/28","url":"https://www.sjsu.edu/people/steven.vukazich/docs/IBC%20EQ%20notes%20S17%20Labs%209%20and%2010.pdf"},{"title":"ASCE 7-22 Site Coefficients for Class C | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Strength Of Materials","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/512409104/ASCE-7-22-Ch-11Com-for-PC-sou"},{"title":"New Site-Specific Ground Motion Requirements of ASCE 7-16","url":"https://cdn.ymaws.com/seaoc.site-ym.com/resource/resmgr/Convention_Proceedings/2017/56R1_PAPER_Kircher_R1.pdf"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf"},{"title":"SEAW Earthquake Engineering Committee Voluntary Use of Multi-Period Response","url":"https://sunfish-daffodil-ebml.squarespace.com/s/White-Paper-EEC-4-2022.pdf"}]},"Based on the web search results, here's what I found regarding the SMS/SD1 and SDS/SD1 relationships in ASCE 7-22:\n\n## Key Relationships\n\nSMS = 1.5 x SDS and SM1 = 1.5 x SD1, representing the Risk-Targeted Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCER) spectral response acceleration parameters.\n\nConversely, design spectral accelerations (SDS and SD1) are obtained by multiplying SMS and SM1 by 2/3 - that is SDS = (2/3) SMS and SD1 = (2/3) SM1.\n\n## Back-Calculation and Site-Specific Applications\n\nThe relationship can be inverted for back-calculation purposes. If you know SDS and SD1 values from a site-specific analysis, you can derive the corresponding SMS and SM1 values by multiplying by 1.5. Conversely, if you have SMS and SM1 values, divide by 1.5 (or multiply by 2/3) to obtain SDS and SD1.\n\n## Site-Specific Context\n\nStructural design in ASCE 7 is performed for earthquake demands that are 2/3 of the MCER response spectra, and these parameters define a design response spectrum for sites of any characteristics. For site-specific ground motion analyses, site-specific requirements recognize that periods of peak response are not always at 0.2 s and 1.0 s and require that SD1 be taken as equal to 2 times the response spectral acceleration at 2 s with greater than 1-s response spectral acceleration."],"durationSeconds":6.913481210999991}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XCHScpvfhG1JpiaLtWW66M","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Bazzurro Cornell 1999 hazard disaggregation seismic hazard\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\",\"url\":\"http://sismologia.ist.utl.pt/~sismologia.daemon/files/Bazurro_and_Cornell_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Southern California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://www.scec.org/publication/407\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America | GeoScienceWorld\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Statewide California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://central.scec.org/publication/407\"},{\"title\":\"Ground motion scenarios consistent with probabilistic seismic hazard disaggregation analysis. Application to Mainland Portugal | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-008-9088-z\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard (1999) | Paolo Bazzurro | 518 Citations\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/papers/disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-3e6zx96xtw\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-Bazzurro-Cornell/907cde3d1c6f6ca8b6e79ded693847be6abe6866\"},{\"title\":\"Researchgate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Paolo_Bazzurro/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard/links/54202dc10cf203f155c2afe3/Disaggregation-of-Seismic-Hazard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard according to Chinese ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_07-0059.PDF\"}]\n\nI've found comprehensive information about the Bazzurro and Cornell 1999 paper on seismic hazard disaggregation. Here's a summary of the search results:\n\n## Publication Details\n\nBazzurro and Cornell's paper \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\" was published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, volume 89(2), pages 501-520, in April 1999.\n\n## Key Contributions\n\nThe paper addresses how probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) integrates over all potential earthquake occurrences and ground motions to estimate the mean frequency of exceedance of any given spectral acceleration at the site, displaying the relative contributions to that hazard from the range of values of magnitude (M), distance (R), and epsilon (ε)—the number of standard deviations from the median ground motion as predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\n## Main Findings\n\nThe paper discusses how the proposed disaggregation procedures, while conceptually similar, differ in several important points that are often not reported by researchers and not appreciated by users, including issues such as the definition of the probability distribution to be disaggregated, different disaggregation techniques, disaggregation of R versus ln R, and the effects of different binning strategies.\n\nThe authors propose improving the disaggregation process by displaying hazard contributions in terms of latitude and longitude as well as M and ε, which permits display directly on maps of faults and enables identification of hazard-dominating scenario events and their association with specific faults, and makes it possible to account for other seismic source characteristics during selection of scenario-based ground-motion time histories for structural analysis.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"a15ca837-f53e-42a9-b28c-21f8b5d6020f","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:11.646Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Bazzurro Cornell 1999 hazard disaggregation seismic hazard","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Jcywn3LBxC7VdJ6YQXKLHk","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard","url":"http://sismologia.ist.utl.pt/~sismologia.daemon/files/Bazurro_and_Cornell_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard.pdf"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Southern California Earthquake Center","url":"https://www.scec.org/publication/407"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America | GeoScienceWorld","url":"https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Statewide California Earthquake Center","url":"https://central.scec.org/publication/407"},{"title":"Ground motion scenarios consistent with probabilistic seismic hazard disaggregation analysis. Application to Mainland Portugal | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-008-9088-z"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard (1999) | Paolo Bazzurro | 518 Citations","url":"https://typeset.io/papers/disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-3e6zx96xtw"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-Bazzurro-Cornell/907cde3d1c6f6ca8b6e79ded693847be6abe6866"},{"title":"Researchgate","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Paolo_Bazzurro/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard/links/54202dc10cf203f155c2afe3/Disaggregation-of-Seismic-Hazard.pdf"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard according to Chinese ...","url":"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_07-0059.PDF"}]},"I've found comprehensive information about the Bazzurro and Cornell 1999 paper on seismic hazard disaggregation. Here's a summary of the search results:\n\n## Publication Details\n\nBazzurro and Cornell's paper \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\" was published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, volume 89(2), pages 501-520, in April 1999.\n\n## Key Contributions\n\nThe paper addresses how probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) integrates over all potential earthquake occurrences and ground motions to estimate the mean frequency of exceedance of any given spectral acceleration at the site, displaying the relative contributions to that hazard from the range of values of magnitude (M), distance (R), and epsilon (ε)—the number of standard deviations from the median ground motion as predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\n## Main Findings\n\nThe paper discusses how the proposed disaggregation procedures, while conceptually similar, differ in several important points that are often not reported by researchers and not appreciated by users, including issues such as the definition of the probability distribution to be disaggregated, different disaggregation techniques, disaggregation of R versus ln R, and the effects of different binning strategies.\n\nThe authors propose improving the disaggregation process by displaying hazard contributions in terms of latitude and longitude as well as M and ε, which permits display directly on maps of faults and enables identification of hazard-dominating scenario events and their association with specific faults, and makes it possible to account for other seismic source characteristics during selection of scenario-based ground-motion time histories for structural analysis."],"durationSeconds":7.674119044999999}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L2dYwSasMVbk69LDhtrGCy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"GISTM 2020 Global Industry Standard Tailings Management seismic design AEP consequence class\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GLOBAL INDUSTRY STANDARD ON TAILINGS MANAGEMENT AUGUST 2020\",\"url\":\"https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.teck.com/sustainability/sustainability-topics/tailings-management/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM): A Complete Overview\",\"url\":\"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hydro.com/globalassets/04-sustainability/2023-07-12-gistm-disclosure-15-1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.srk.com/en/global-tailings-standard\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://geosyntec.com/markets/mining/gistm\"},{\"title\":\"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)\",\"url\":\"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php\"},{\"title\":\"Reviewing Principles 2 & 3 of the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management - The Insight Blog from Insight Terra\",\"url\":\"https://www.insightterra.com/blog-posts/gistm-principles-2-and-3\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found information about the GISTM 2020 (Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management) and its approach to seismic design and consequence classification. Here are the key findings:\n\n## GISTM 2020 Overview\n\nThe GISTM was launched in August 2020 by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), the Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI), and the International Council on Mining and Metals (ICMM), and defines 15 principles and 77 requirements that apply to the entire lifecycle of a tailings facility.\n\n## Seismic Design\n\nThe knowledge base required under the standard should include data on seismicity. Additionally, operators such as BHP, Teck, and Antofagasta report high alignment with GISTM, integrating seismic-resistant design codes and filtered tailings systems.\n\n## Consequence Classification\n\nThe consequence of failure classification of the tailings facility is determined by assessing the downstream conditions and selecting the classification corresponding to the highest Consequence Classification for each category. The Standard contains consequence classification tables in Annex 2.\n\nThe search results reference consequence classifications including \"Extreme\" and \"Very High\" categories, but the specific AEP (Annual Exceedance Probability) values and detailed consequence class definitions from Annex 2 are not fully detailed in the publicly available excerpts from the search results. You may need to consult the full GISTM document directly for the specific AEP threshold values associated with each consequence class.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"b49f6bad-d065-4143-aa30-dbf2e2e2e239","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:12.101Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"GISTM 2020 Global Industry Standard Tailings Management seismic design AEP consequence class","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GWYHgh8x9SWKMLcxsY8UZA","content":[{"title":"GLOBAL INDUSTRY STANDARD ON TAILINGS MANAGEMENT AUGUST 2020","url":"https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf"},{"title":"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.teck.com/sustainability/sustainability-topics/tailings-management/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management/"},{"title":"Understanding the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM): A Complete Overview","url":"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://www.hydro.com/globalassets/04-sustainability/2023-07-12-gistm-disclosure-15-1.pdf"},{"title":"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.srk.com/en/global-tailings-standard"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://geosyntec.com/markets/mining/gistm"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php"},{"title":"Reviewing Principles 2 & 3 of the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management - The Insight Blog from Insight Terra","url":"https://www.insightterra.com/blog-posts/gistm-principles-2-and-3"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found information about the GISTM 2020 (Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management) and its approach to seismic design and consequence classification. Here are the key findings:\n\n## GISTM 2020 Overview\n\nThe GISTM was launched in August 2020 by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), the Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI), and the International Council on Mining and Metals (ICMM), and defines 15 principles and 77 requirements that apply to the entire lifecycle of a tailings facility.\n\n## Seismic Design\n\nThe knowledge base required under the standard should include data on seismicity. Additionally, operators such as BHP, Teck, and Antofagasta report high alignment with GISTM, integrating seismic-resistant design codes and filtered tailings systems.\n\n## Consequence Classification\n\nThe consequence of failure classification of the tailings facility is determined by assessing the downstream conditions and selecting the classification corresponding to the highest Consequence Classification for each category. The Standard contains consequence classification tables in Annex 2.\n\nThe search results reference consequence classifications including \"Extreme\" and \"Very High\" categories, but the specific AEP (Annual Exceedance Probability) values and detailed consequence class definitions from Annex 2 are not fully detailed in the publicly available excerpts from the search results. You may need to consult the full GISTM document directly for the specific AEP threshold values associated with each consequence class."],"durationSeconds":6.800740143999996}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AA7CQiFuPjBXMKTcRLwtjX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Cornell 1968 engineering seismic risk analysis PSHA annual frequency exceedance hazard integral\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Cornell_1968.pdf\",\"url\":\"https://courses.washington.edu/ess502/Cornell_1968.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) A Primer\",\"url\":\"https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Why is Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) still used? - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0031920116303016\"},{\"title\":\"A Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis Method Incorporating Physics-Based Simulation and Ground Motion Prediction Equation | International Journal of Disaster Risk Science\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13753-025-00640-7\"},{\"title\":\"Engineering Seismic Risk Analysis | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/cornell-1968\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Probabilistic seismic hazard analysis: Early history\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/128565223/Probabilistic_seismic_hazard_analysis_Early_history\"},{\"title\":\"Cornell, C.A. (1968): “Engineering Seismic Risk Analysis”,\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciepub.com/reference/124847\"},{\"title\":\"1 Chapter 1 Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis:\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Katsuichiro-Goda/publication/257875995_Probabilistic_seismic_hazard_analysis_of_civil_infrastructure/links/5a2498104585155dd41eb2f8/Probabilistic-seismic-hazard-analysis-of-civil-infrastructure.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"UNDERSTANDING SEISMIC HAZARD AND RISK: A GAP BETWEEN\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_S27-001.PDF\"},{\"title\":\"An Integrated Uncertainty Quantification Framework for Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis | ASCE-ASME Journal of Risk and Uncertainty in Engineering Systems, Part A: Civil Engineering | Vol 9, No 2\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/AJRUA6.RUENG-1035\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I've found relevant information about Cornell's 1968 work on engineering seismic risk analysis. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Cornell 1968 - Engineering Seismic Risk Analysis\n\nCornell's 1968 paper introduced a method for the evaluation of seismic risk at the site of an engineering project. The results are expressed in terms of a ground motion parameter (such as peak acceleration) versus average return period.\n\nThe method incorporates the influence of all potential sources of earthquakes and the average activity rates assigned to them, with computational ease for arbitrary geographical relationships between the site and potential point, line, or areal sources.\n\n## Connection to PSHA and Annual Frequency of Exceedance\n\nThe goal of probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) is to quantify the rate (or probability) of exceeding various ground-motion levels at a site given all possible earthquakes, and the numerical/analytical approach to PSHA was first formalized by Cornell (1968).\n\nProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis is the evaluation of annual frequencies of exceedence of ground motion levels at a site, and the result of a PSHA is a seismic hazard curve showing annual frequency of exceedence versus ground motion amplitude or a uniform hazard spectrum.\n\nThe Cornell method forms the mathematical foundation for modern PSHA, which is widely used today to evaluate seismic hazards and inform engineering design standards.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"7d279b1e-5af3-452e-85fb-345cb733e8c2","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:14.376Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Cornell 1968 engineering seismic risk analysis PSHA annual frequency exceedance hazard integral","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01WMQHJDVa5NPt22Dupi4HW8","content":[{"title":"Cornell_1968.pdf","url":"https://courses.washington.edu/ess502/Cornell_1968.pdf"},{"title":"Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) A Primer","url":"https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf"},{"title":"Why is Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) still used? - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0031920116303016"},{"title":"A Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis Method Incorporating Physics-Based Simulation and Ground Motion Prediction Equation | International Journal of Disaster Risk Science","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13753-025-00640-7"},{"title":"Engineering Seismic Risk Analysis | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement","url":"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/cornell-1968"},{"title":"(PDF) Probabilistic seismic hazard analysis: Early history","url":"https://www.academia.edu/128565223/Probabilistic_seismic_hazard_analysis_Early_history"},{"title":"Cornell, C.A. (1968): “Engineering Seismic Risk Analysis”,","url":"https://www.sciepub.com/reference/124847"},{"title":"1 Chapter 1 Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis:","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Katsuichiro-Goda/publication/257875995_Probabilistic_seismic_hazard_analysis_of_civil_infrastructure/links/5a2498104585155dd41eb2f8/Probabilistic-seismic-hazard-analysis-of-civil-infrastructure.pdf"},{"title":"UNDERSTANDING SEISMIC HAZARD AND RISK: A GAP BETWEEN","url":"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_S27-001.PDF"},{"title":"An Integrated Uncertainty Quantification Framework for Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis | ASCE-ASME Journal of Risk and Uncertainty in Engineering Systems, Part A: Civil Engineering | Vol 9, No 2","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/AJRUA6.RUENG-1035"}]},"Based on the search results, I've found relevant information about Cornell's 1968 work on engineering seismic risk analysis. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Cornell 1968 - Engineering Seismic Risk Analysis\n\nCornell's 1968 paper introduced a method for the evaluation of seismic risk at the site of an engineering project. The results are expressed in terms of a ground motion parameter (such as peak acceleration) versus average return period.\n\nThe method incorporates the influence of all potential sources of earthquakes and the average activity rates assigned to them, with computational ease for arbitrary geographical relationships between the site and potential point, line, or areal sources.\n\n## Connection to PSHA and Annual Frequency of Exceedance\n\nThe goal of probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) is to quantify the rate (or probability) of exceeding various ground-motion levels at a site given all possible earthquakes, and the numerical/analytical approach to PSHA was first formalized by Cornell (1968).\n\nProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis is the evaluation of annual frequencies of exceedence of ground motion levels at a site, and the result of a PSHA is a seismic hazard curve showing annual frequency of exceedence versus ground motion amplitude or a uniform hazard spectrum.\n\nThe Cornell method forms the mathematical foundation for modern PSHA, which is widely used today to evaluate seismic hazards and inform engineering design standards."],"durationSeconds":7.205518859999996}}
