{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pj3z2tXBahsGhoaVsYYEHx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ASCE 7 site-specific SDS \"0.9\" maximum spectral acceleration \"0.2 to 5 seconds\" formula 21.4\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4\",\"url\":\"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Load Calculation as per ASCE 7-16\",\"url\":\"https://rcenggstudios.com/seismic-load-calculation-as-per-asce-7-16/\"},{\"title\":\"Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters and Design Spectral Acceleration Parameters per ASCE 7-16 with ideCAD\",\"url\":\"https://help.idecad.com/ideCAD/11-4-4-11-4-5-spectral-response-acceleration-param\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Base Shear Calculator\",\"url\":\"https://www.buildingsguide.com/calculators/structural/IBC2006E/\"},{\"title\":\"Peak Ground acceleration | Eng-Tips\",\"url\":\"https://www.eng-tips.com/threads/peak-ground-acceleration.355253/\"},{\"title\":\"Response spectrum curves (ASCE)\",\"url\":\"https://www.graitec.com/Help/Advance_Design/En/Response_spectrum_curves_(ASCE).htm\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-16 Seismic Load Calculation Example Using Equivalent Lateral Force Procedure | SkyCiv Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://skyciv.com/docs/tech-notes/loading/asce-7-16-seismic-load-calculation-example/\"},{\"title\":\"New Site-Specific Ground Motion Requirements of ASCE 7-16\",\"url\":\"https://cdn.ymaws.com/seaoc.site-ym.com/resource/resmgr/Convention_Proceedings/2017/56R1_PAPER_Kircher_R1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Winter 2014 | PCI Journal 60 T he ASCE 7 standard Minimum Design Loads for\",\"url\":\"https://www.pci.org/PCI_Docs/Publications/PCI%20Journal/2014/Winter/Significant%20changes%20from%20ASCE%207-05%20to%20ASCE%207-10%20part%201%20-%20Seismic%20design%20provisions.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found information about ASCE 7 site-specific design response spectra and related procedures. The search results reference site-specific response spectrum procedures set forth in Chapter 21 of ASCE 7.\n\nThe design spectral response acceleration at any period is determined from Equation 21.3-1, which relates to site-specific ground motion analysis.\n\nRegarding the specific parameters you mentioned (SDS maximum of 0.9, periods of 0.2 to 5 seconds, and Formula 21.4), the search results indicate that Chapter 21 of ASCE 7 contains the site-specific ground motion requirements, but the exact details of Formula 21.4 are not fully displayed in these search results. \n\nThe deterministic spectral response acceleration at each period is calculated as an 84th-percentile 5% damped spectral response acceleration in the direction of maximum horizontal response computed at that period, with the largest such acceleration calculated for the characteristic earthquakes on all known active faults within the region being used.\n\nIf you need the specific formula 21.4 details or more information about the exact constraints related to the 0.9 maximum SDS value, you may need to consult the full ASCE 7 standard document directly, as these specific technical details are not fully captured in the available web sources.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"c39995c1-de3e-4569-8f50-2b98bed2c35c","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:28.846Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ASCE 7 site-specific SDS \"0.9\" maximum spectral acceleration \"0.2 to 5 seconds\" formula 21.4","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017Jc8rKYGveLEKRvoZFqNvV","content":[{"title":"Design Example 1 ■ Design Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters §11.4","url":"https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S211-Sample.pdf"},{"title":"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1"},{"title":"Seismic Load Calculation as per ASCE 7-16","url":"https://rcenggstudios.com/seismic-load-calculation-as-per-asce-7-16/"},{"title":"Spectral Response Acceleration Parameters and Design Spectral Acceleration Parameters per ASCE 7-16 with ideCAD","url":"https://help.idecad.com/ideCAD/11-4-4-11-4-5-spectral-response-acceleration-param"},{"title":"Seismic Base Shear Calculator","url":"https://www.buildingsguide.com/calculators/structural/IBC2006E/"},{"title":"Peak Ground acceleration | Eng-Tips","url":"https://www.eng-tips.com/threads/peak-ground-acceleration.355253/"},{"title":"Response spectrum curves (ASCE)","url":"https://www.graitec.com/Help/Advance_Design/En/Response_spectrum_curves_(ASCE).htm"},{"title":"ASCE 7-16 Seismic Load Calculation Example Using Equivalent Lateral Force Procedure | SkyCiv Engineering","url":"https://skyciv.com/docs/tech-notes/loading/asce-7-16-seismic-load-calculation-example/"},{"title":"New Site-Specific Ground Motion Requirements of ASCE 7-16","url":"https://cdn.ymaws.com/seaoc.site-ym.com/resource/resmgr/Convention_Proceedings/2017/56R1_PAPER_Kircher_R1.pdf"},{"title":"Winter 2014 | PCI Journal 60 T he ASCE 7 standard Minimum Design Loads for","url":"https://www.pci.org/PCI_Docs/Publications/PCI%20Journal/2014/Winter/Significant%20changes%20from%20ASCE%207-05%20to%20ASCE%207-10%20part%201%20-%20Seismic%20design%20provisions.pdf"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found information about ASCE 7 site-specific design response spectra and related procedures. The search results reference site-specific response spectrum procedures set forth in Chapter 21 of ASCE 7.\n\nThe design spectral response acceleration at any period is determined from Equation 21.3-1, which relates to site-specific ground motion analysis.\n\nRegarding the specific parameters you mentioned (SDS maximum of 0.9, periods of 0.2 to 5 seconds, and Formula 21.4), the search results indicate that Chapter 21 of ASCE 7 contains the site-specific ground motion requirements, but the exact details of Formula 21.4 are not fully displayed in these search results. \n\nThe deterministic spectral response acceleration at each period is calculated as an 84th-percentile 5% damped spectral response acceleration in the direction of maximum horizontal response computed at that period, with the largest such acceleration calculated for the characteristic earthquakes on all known active faults within the region being used.\n\nIf you need the specific formula 21.4 details or more information about the exact constraints related to the 0.9 maximum SDS value, you may need to consult the full ASCE 7 standard document directly, as these specific technical details are not fully captured in the available web sources."],"durationSeconds":6.810830407000001}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Pqe7HhwPLY5WoCvNcECDP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CDA 2021 Canadian Dam Association tailings safety evaluation earthquake seismic criteria consequence\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CDA Guidance Documents - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)\",\"url\":\"https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents\"},{\"title\":\"Dam Safety Publications - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)\",\"url\":\"https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents/dam-safety-publications\"},{\"title\":\"ENVIRONMENTAL ASSESSMENT APPLICATION AND ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT STATEMENT\",\"url\":\"https://iaac-aeic.gc.ca/050/documents/p80093/120977E.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Canadian Dam Association (CDA) consequence classification ratings for dams - Open Government\",\"url\":\"https://open.alberta.ca/publications/cda-consequence-classification-ratings-for-dams\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Dam Breach Analysis - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)\",\"url\":\"https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents/tailings-dam-breach-analysis\"},{\"title\":\"Schematic of the Canadian Dam Association (CDA) Tailings Dam Breach... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-Canadian-Dam-Association-CDA-Tailings-Dam-Breach-Analysis-TDBA_fig3_353090918\"},{\"title\":\"CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analyses V. Martin\",\"url\":\"https://www.knightpiesold.com/sites/en/assets/File/ICOLD%202019%20-%20CDA%20Tailings%20Dam%20Breach.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings Dam Breach Analysis Technical Bulletin 2021 - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)\",\"url\":\"https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021\"},{\"title\":\"TAILINGS DAMS DESIGN CRITERIA AND SAFETY EVALUATIONS AT CLOSURE\",\"url\":\"https://open.library.ubc.ca/media/stream/pdf/59367/1.0042456/1\"},{\"title\":\"Tailings facility dam safety review (DSR) - Tailings storage dam management & regulations - Barr\",\"url\":\"https://www.barr.com/insights/how-safe-is-your-tailings-dam/\"}]\n\nBased on my search results, here is information about CDA 2021 Canadian Dam Association tailings safety evaluation, earthquake seismic criteria, and consequence classification:\n\n## CDA 2021 Tailings Dam Breach Analysis\n\nThe CDA Technical Bulletin published in 2021 is intended to provide dam safety professionals with guidance for conducting a Tailings Dam Breach Analysis (TDBA). The results of the analysis are typically presented on inundation/runout maps and could be used for multiple purposes including dam safety management, hazard and consequence evaluation, emergency preparation and response planning, failure mitigation planning, and mine closure and dam decommissioning planning.\n\n## Seismic Design Criteria\n\nThe Canadian Dam Association Dam Safety Guidelines state that for tailings dams, the minimum design criteria corresponds to that of the 1/2,475 year return period seismic event (i.e. a 2% probability of exceedance in 50 years) for a Dam Hazard Classification of HIGH or lower. For HIGH dam classification during construction and operational phases, the EDGM (Earthquake Design Ground Motion) is based on a 1/2,475 year return period seismic event.\n\n## Consequence Classification\n\nDam safety uses five classifications for dams based on evaluation of the potential downstream consequences of failure in terms of three categories, namely: loss of life; environment and cultural values; and infrastructure and economics.\n\n## Key Tailings-Specific Considerations\n\nGuidelines for dam breach studies are available for water retaining dams, but none of these guidelines deal with the hydrodynamic, geotechnical and rheological considerations specific to tailings outflows.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"26fc8125-6138-450b-8e8a-09239b8d9e2f","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:29.959Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CDA 2021 Canadian Dam Association tailings safety evaluation earthquake seismic criteria consequence","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_018H6q1EKux76pjy4cH9VW7t","content":[{"title":"CDA Guidance Documents - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)","url":"https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents"},{"title":"Dam Safety Publications - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)","url":"https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents/dam-safety-publications"},{"title":"ENVIRONMENTAL ASSESSMENT APPLICATION AND ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT STATEMENT","url":"https://iaac-aeic.gc.ca/050/documents/p80093/120977E.pdf"},{"title":"Canadian Dam Association (CDA) consequence classification ratings for dams - Open Government","url":"https://open.alberta.ca/publications/cda-consequence-classification-ratings-for-dams"},{"title":"Tailings Dam Breach Analysis - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)","url":"https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents/tailings-dam-breach-analysis"},{"title":"Schematic of the Canadian Dam Association (CDA) Tailings Dam Breach... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-Canadian-Dam-Association-CDA-Tailings-Dam-Breach-Analysis-TDBA_fig3_353090918"},{"title":"CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analyses V. Martin","url":"https://www.knightpiesold.com/sites/en/assets/File/ICOLD%202019%20-%20CDA%20Tailings%20Dam%20Breach.pdf"},{"title":"Tailings Dam Breach Analysis Technical Bulletin 2021 - Canadian Dam Association (CDA-ACB)","url":"https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021"},{"title":"TAILINGS DAMS DESIGN CRITERIA AND SAFETY EVALUATIONS AT CLOSURE","url":"https://open.library.ubc.ca/media/stream/pdf/59367/1.0042456/1"},{"title":"Tailings facility dam safety review (DSR) - Tailings storage dam management & regulations - Barr","url":"https://www.barr.com/insights/how-safe-is-your-tailings-dam/"}]},"Based on my search results, here is information about CDA 2021 Canadian Dam Association tailings safety evaluation, earthquake seismic criteria, and consequence classification:\n\n## CDA 2021 Tailings Dam Breach Analysis\n\nThe CDA Technical Bulletin published in 2021 is intended to provide dam safety professionals with guidance for conducting a Tailings Dam Breach Analysis (TDBA). The results of the analysis are typically presented on inundation/runout maps and could be used for multiple purposes including dam safety management, hazard and consequence evaluation, emergency preparation and response planning, failure mitigation planning, and mine closure and dam decommissioning planning.\n\n## Seismic Design Criteria\n\nThe Canadian Dam Association Dam Safety Guidelines state that for tailings dams, the minimum design criteria corresponds to that of the 1/2,475 year return period seismic event (i.e. a 2% probability of exceedance in 50 years) for a Dam Hazard Classification of HIGH or lower. For HIGH dam classification during construction and operational phases, the EDGM (Earthquake Design Ground Motion) is based on a 1/2,475 year return period seismic event.\n\n## Consequence Classification\n\nDam safety uses five classifications for dams based on evaluation of the potential downstream consequences of failure in terms of three categories, namely: loss of life; environment and cultural values; and infrastructure and economics.\n\n## Key Tailings-Specific Considerations\n\nGuidelines for dam breach studies are available for water retaining dams, but none of these guidelines deal with the hydrodynamic, geotechnical and rheological considerations specific to tailings outflows."],"durationSeconds":7.499560973999992}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYsELT8wg9aymqXMndfWWd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NEHRP site class A B C D E F Vs30 thresholds 1500 760 360 180 m/s seismic\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"E F D C B A Seismic Site Classification (Vs30-m or Vs100-ft) Site Class\",\"url\":\"http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mapping NEHRP VS30 site classes | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes\"},{\"title\":\"Estimating V ¢ s(30) (or NEHRP Site Classes) from ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"README_Boise_Area_NEHRP_Site_Class_Map.pdf\",\"url\":\"https://www.idahogeology.org/pub/Digital_Data/Digital_Databases/DD5_Boise_NEHRP_Liquefaction/README_Boise_Area_NEHRP_Site_Class_Map.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"NEHRP site classes and Vs30 ranges used in Figure 6. | Download Table\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/NEHRP-site-classes-and-Vs30-ranges-used-in-Figure-6_tbl1_267267775\"},{\"title\":\"NEHRP Site Class and Liquefaction Susceptibility Maps for ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.idahogeology.org/pub/Digital_Data/Digital_Databases/DD8_WoodRiverArea_NEHRP_Liquefaction/AppendixA_06_08_2013.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Estimating V̄s(30) (or NEHRP site classes) from shallow velocity models (depths < 30 m)\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/estimating-vs30-or-nehrp-site-classes-shallow-velocity-models-depths-30-m\"},{\"title\":\"New site coefficients and site classification system used in recent building seismic code provisions\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70022904\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Mapping NEHRP V S30 Site Classes\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/18608217/Mapping_NEHRP_V_S30_Site_Classes\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Design Requirements, H-18-8\",\"url\":\"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/H-18-8.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about NEHRP site classes and their relationship to shear wave velocity (Vs30). Here's what the results reveal:\n\n## NEHRP Site Classes Overview\n\nSite classes A and B correspond to hard rock and rock, Site Class C corresponds to soft rock and very stiff/very dense soil, and Site Classes D and E correspond to stiff soil and soft soil. A sixth site class, F, is defined for soils requiring site-specific evaluations.\n\n## Vs30 Velocity Ranges\n\nWhile the search results reference NEHRP site classification based on Vs30 values, the specific numerical thresholds you mentioned (1500, 760, 360, 180 m/s) are not explicitly detailed in these particular results. However, the search results do include references to specific ranges:\n\n- Units of Holocene and Pleistocene age generally classify as site class C with Vs30 values between 490-360 m/s\n- Vs30 values ranging from 286 m/sec (NEHRP-D) to 849 m/sec (NEHRP-B)\n\nThe search results indicate that Vs30 is a widely used parameter for classifying sites to predict their potential to amplify seismic shaking, and that site classes (A-F) are determined in engineering studies with geotechnical properties of earth materials within 100 feet (30 m) of the ground surface.\n\nFor the exact threshold values you're seeking, you may want to consult the ASCE 7 Building Code standards or NEHRP documentation directly, as the specific numerical boundaries weren't fully detailed in these search results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"43ab326f-7500-454d-8ca6-45799b33b9d0","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:30.593Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"NEHRP site class A B C D E F Vs30 thresholds 1500 760 360 180 m/s seismic","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GABxrRqGkk8Eh8pYjA8QUr","content":[{"title":"E F D C B A Seismic Site Classification (Vs30-m or Vs100-ft) Site Class","url":"http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf"},{"title":"Mapping NEHRP VS30 site classes | U.S. Geological Survey","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes"},{"title":"Estimating V ¢ s(30) (or NEHRP Site Classes) from ...","url":"https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf"},{"title":"README_Boise_Area_NEHRP_Site_Class_Map.pdf","url":"https://www.idahogeology.org/pub/Digital_Data/Digital_Databases/DD5_Boise_NEHRP_Liquefaction/README_Boise_Area_NEHRP_Site_Class_Map.pdf"},{"title":"NEHRP site classes and Vs30 ranges used in Figure 6. | Download Table","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/NEHRP-site-classes-and-Vs30-ranges-used-in-Figure-6_tbl1_267267775"},{"title":"NEHRP Site Class and Liquefaction Susceptibility Maps for ...","url":"https://www.idahogeology.org/pub/Digital_Data/Digital_Databases/DD8_WoodRiverArea_NEHRP_Liquefaction/AppendixA_06_08_2013.pdf"},{"title":"Estimating V̄s(30) (or NEHRP site classes) from shallow velocity models (depths < 30 m)","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/estimating-vs30-or-nehrp-site-classes-shallow-velocity-models-depths-30-m"},{"title":"New site coefficients and site classification system used in recent building seismic code provisions","url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70022904"},{"title":"(PDF) Mapping NEHRP V S30 Site Classes","url":"https://www.academia.edu/18608217/Mapping_NEHRP_V_S30_Site_Classes"},{"title":"Seismic Design Requirements, H-18-8","url":"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/H-18-8.pdf"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about NEHRP site classes and their relationship to shear wave velocity (Vs30). Here's what the results reveal:\n\n## NEHRP Site Classes Overview\n\nSite classes A and B correspond to hard rock and rock, Site Class C corresponds to soft rock and very stiff/very dense soil, and Site Classes D and E correspond to stiff soil and soft soil. A sixth site class, F, is defined for soils requiring site-specific evaluations.\n\n## Vs30 Velocity Ranges\n\nWhile the search results reference NEHRP site classification based on Vs30 values, the specific numerical thresholds you mentioned (1500, 760, 360, 180 m/s) are not explicitly detailed in these particular results. However, the search results do include references to specific ranges:\n\n- Units of Holocene and Pleistocene age generally classify as site class C with Vs30 values between 490-360 m/s\n- Vs30 values ranging from 286 m/sec (NEHRP-D) to 849 m/sec (NEHRP-B)\n\nThe search results indicate that Vs30 is a widely used parameter for classifying sites to predict their potential to amplify seismic shaking, and that site classes (A-F) are determined in engineering studies with geotechnical properties of earth materials within 100 feet (30 m) of the ground surface.\n\nFor the exact threshold values you're seeking, you may want to consult the ASCE 7 Building Code standards or NEHRP documentation directly, as the specific numerical boundaries weren't fully detailed in these search results."],"durationSeconds":6.6753056389999985}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gqy3kE7GP2k18VkDtcxJFp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Stewart 2020 amplification model Vs30 site effects nonlinear ground motion GMPE\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Semi-Empirical Nonlinear Site Amplification from NGA-West2 Data and Simulations\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/271384852_Semi-Empirical_Nonlinear_Site_Amplification_from_NGA-West2_Data_and_Simulations\"},{\"title\":\"Two-stage nonlinear site amplification modeling for Japan with VS30 and fundamental frequency dependency - Dong Youp Kwak, Emel Seyhan, 2020\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/8755293020907920\"},{\"title\":\"Site amplifications and deamplifications versus VS30 for different... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Site-amplifications-and-deamplifications-versus-VS30-for-different-periods-A-T-001-s_fig3_363871417\"},{\"title\":\"Two-stage nonlinear site amplification modeling for Japan with V S 30 and fundamental frequency dependency | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341826986_Two-stage_nonlinear_site_amplification_modeling_for_Japan_with_V_S_30_and_fundamental_frequency_dependency\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Site Amplification as Function of 30m Shear ...\",\"url\":\"https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SCEC2020 Poster #221: Region-specific Fourier-based site amplification modeling | Statewide California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://central.scec.org/meetings/2020/am/poster/221\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Machine learning based ground motion site amplification prediction\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2023.1053085/full\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Comparison of VS30 and Site Period as Site-Effect Parameters in Response Spectral Ground-Motion Prediction Equations\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258761436_A_Comparison_of_VS30_and_Site_Period_as_Site-Effect_Parameters_in_Response_Spectral_Ground-Motion_Prediction_Equations\"},{\"title\":\"nonlinear site effect model for the horizontal component of ground-motion prediction equations using VS30 derived from the KiK-net and K-NET sites in Japan | Research Square\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-4845064/v1\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Rock and Stiff-Soil Site Amplification: Dependency on VS30 and Kappa ( 0)\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/260491098_Rock_and_Stiff-Soil_Site_Amplification_Dependency_on_VS30_and_Kappa_0\"}]\n\nThe search results provide relevant information about site amplification models, Vs30, and ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs). Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Results\n\n**Stewart and Related Work on Site Amplification:**\n\nStewart et al. 2013 conducted work on nonlinear site amplification models, and Seyhan and Stewart (2014) developed semi-empirical nonlinear site amplification from NGA-West2 data and simulations.\n\n**Vs30-Based Site Effects and Nonlinearity:**\n\nNonlinearity decreases with increasing Vs30, generally becoming statistically insignificant for relatively stiff sites, and nonlinearity decreases as period increases, being statistically significant only for T≤∼1s, except for the softest soil sites (Vs30 <200 m/s).\n\n**Recent 2020 Models:**\n\nA two-stage nonlinear site amplification model was developed that considers both the sediment depth and the fundamental frequency of the soil, adopting a non-reference site approach with empirical site amplification ratios for 1591 Kyoshin and Kiban Kyoshin network sites.\n\n**GMPE Integration:**\n\nModels show that the residual associated with GMPEs that only include Vs30 as a site parameter decreases considerably when including the observed values of fundamental frequency at the sites, mainly for spectral periods >0.1 s.\n\nThe search results indicate active research in developing improved nonlinear site amplification models that supplement Vs30 with additional parameters like fundamental frequency and sediment depth for better GMPE predictions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"23dcf297-59a7-4a78-9a00-58628a58a9f7","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:30.714Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Stewart 2020 amplification model Vs30 site effects nonlinear ground motion GMPE","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Wdy8BsRSbB6MUDneCD2iJJ","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Semi-Empirical Nonlinear Site Amplification from NGA-West2 Data and Simulations","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/271384852_Semi-Empirical_Nonlinear_Site_Amplification_from_NGA-West2_Data_and_Simulations"},{"title":"Two-stage nonlinear site amplification modeling for Japan with VS30 and fundamental frequency dependency - Dong Youp Kwak, Emel Seyhan, 2020","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/8755293020907920"},{"title":"Site amplifications and deamplifications versus VS30 for different... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Site-amplifications-and-deamplifications-versus-VS30-for-different-periods-A-T-001-s_fig3_363871417"},{"title":"Two-stage nonlinear site amplification modeling for Japan with V S 30 and fundamental frequency dependency | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341826986_Two-stage_nonlinear_site_amplification_modeling_for_Japan_with_V_S_30_and_fundamental_frequency_dependency"},{"title":"Nonlinear Site Amplification as Function of 30m Shear ...","url":"https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf"},{"title":"SCEC2020 Poster #221: Region-specific Fourier-based site amplification modeling | Statewide California Earthquake Center","url":"https://central.scec.org/meetings/2020/am/poster/221"},{"title":"Frontiers | Machine learning based ground motion site amplification prediction","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2023.1053085/full"},{"title":"(PDF) A Comparison of VS30 and Site Period as Site-Effect Parameters in Response Spectral Ground-Motion Prediction Equations","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258761436_A_Comparison_of_VS30_and_Site_Period_as_Site-Effect_Parameters_in_Response_Spectral_Ground-Motion_Prediction_Equations"},{"title":"nonlinear site effect model for the horizontal component of ground-motion prediction equations using VS30 derived from the KiK-net and K-NET sites in Japan | Research Square","url":"https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-4845064/v1"},{"title":"(PDF) Rock and Stiff-Soil Site Amplification: Dependency on VS30 and Kappa ( 0)","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/260491098_Rock_and_Stiff-Soil_Site_Amplification_Dependency_on_VS30_and_Kappa_0"}]},"The search results provide relevant information about site amplification models, Vs30, and ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs). Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Results\n\n**Stewart and Related Work on Site Amplification:**\n\nStewart et al. 2013 conducted work on nonlinear site amplification models, and Seyhan and Stewart (2014) developed semi-empirical nonlinear site amplification from NGA-West2 data and simulations.\n\n**Vs30-Based Site Effects and Nonlinearity:**\n\nNonlinearity decreases with increasing Vs30, generally becoming statistically insignificant for relatively stiff sites, and nonlinearity decreases as period increases, being statistically significant only for T≤∼1s, except for the softest soil sites (Vs30 <200 m/s).\n\n**Recent 2020 Models:**\n\nA two-stage nonlinear site amplification model was developed that considers both the sediment depth and the fundamental frequency of the soil, adopting a non-reference site approach with empirical site amplification ratios for 1591 Kyoshin and Kiban Kyoshin network sites.\n\n**GMPE Integration:**\n\nModels show that the residual associated with GMPEs that only include Vs30 as a site parameter decreases considerably when including the observed values of fundamental frequency at the sites, mainly for spectral periods >0.1 s.\n\nThe search results indicate active research in developing improved nonlinear site amplification models that supplement Vs30 with additional parameters like fundamental frequency and sediment depth for better GMPE predictions."],"durationSeconds":7.412111072}}
