{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LHZJmUMvimG9rWjBH6u9T2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ANCOLD 2019 guidelines tailings dams seismic criteria OBE SEE 1/500 1/1000 annual exceedance probability\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"AUSTRALIAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS\",\"url\":\"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/ancold-2012.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Guidelines on Tailings Dams-Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure-REVISION 1 (2019) - HARDCOPY - ANCOLD\",\"url\":\"https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Guidelines and recommendations on minimum factors of safety for slope stability of tailings dams\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347741840_Guidelines_and_recommendations_on_minimum_factors_of_safety_for_slope_stability_of_tailings_dams\"},{\"title\":\"ANCOLD Tailings Dam Guideline Working Party\",\"url\":\"https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ANCOLD-response-combined-min.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ESG briefing: Tailings dams Escondida June 2019\",\"url\":\"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2019/190607_esgbriefingtailingsdams.pdf?la=en\"},{\"title\":\"SELECTING SEISMIC PARAMETERS FOR LARGE DAMS Guidelines\",\"url\":\"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Guidelines Archives - ANCOLD\",\"url\":\"https://ancold.org.au/product-category/guidelines/\"},{\"title\":\"Ancold - Guidelines On Tailings Dam | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/581384623/Ancold-guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam\"},{\"title\":\"GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS\",\"url\":\"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about ANCOLD 2019 guidelines on seismic criteria for tailings dams:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE):**\nThe OBE is that level of ground motion at the dam site which leads to only minor damage.\n\n**Safe Earthquake (SEE) and Annual Exceedance Probability:**\nThe search results show that where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the dam, the design may need to consider a 1:10,000 AEP earthquake, unless an appropriate risk assessment is undertaken to confirm that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower earthquake loading. Additionally, for the post-closure case, ANCOLD recommend that all tailings dams are evaluated for stability using probabilistic ground motion from a 1 in 10,000 AEP event. This is due to the long life expectation of tailings dams in the post-closure phase.\n\n**2019 Updates:**\nSome of the important matters covered in this revision include reinforcement of the need for robust management practices, updates on earthquake considerations to align with the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019) and additional information on static liquefaction.\n\nThe search results indicate that the 2019 ANCOLD guidelines addendum specifically addresses seismic design criteria for tailings dams with reference to annual exceedance probabilities, though the full detailed technical specifications appear to be contained in the complete guideline documents referenced in the search results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"eaba32eb-2a0a-4900-94aa-8ce7c594a111","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:51.582Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ANCOLD 2019 guidelines tailings dams seismic criteria OBE SEE 1/500 1/1000 annual exceedance probability","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012hCfDiXhZeGLczcPoffNXT","content":[{"title":"AUSTRALIAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE ON LARGE DAMS GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS","url":"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf"},{"title":"GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS","url":"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/ancold-2012.pdf"},{"title":"Guidelines on Tailings Dams-Planning, Design, Construction, Operation and Closure-REVISION 1 (2019) - HARDCOPY - ANCOLD","url":"https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/"},{"title":"(PDF) Guidelines and recommendations on minimum factors of safety for slope stability of tailings dams","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347741840_Guidelines_and_recommendations_on_minimum_factors_of_safety_for_slope_stability_of_tailings_dams"},{"title":"ANCOLD Tailings Dam Guideline Working Party","url":"https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ANCOLD-response-combined-min.pdf"},{"title":"ESG briefing: Tailings dams Escondida June 2019","url":"https://www.bhp.com/-/media/documents/media/reports-and-presentations/2019/190607_esgbriefingtailingsdams.pdf?la=en"},{"title":"SELECTING SEISMIC PARAMETERS FOR LARGE DAMS Guidelines","url":"https://www.icoldchile.cl/boletines/148.pdf"},{"title":"Guidelines Archives - ANCOLD","url":"https://ancold.org.au/product-category/guidelines/"},{"title":"Ancold - Guidelines On Tailings Dam | PDF","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/581384623/Ancold-guidelines-on-Tailings-Dam"},{"title":"GUIDELINES ON TAILINGS DAMS","url":"https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019.pdf"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found relevant information about ANCOLD 2019 guidelines on seismic criteria for tailings dams:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE):**\nThe OBE is that level of ground motion at the dam site which leads to only minor damage.\n\n**Safe Earthquake (SEE) and Annual Exceedance Probability:**\nThe search results show that where liquefaction of tailings is likely to affect the stability of the dam, the design may need to consider a 1:10,000 AEP earthquake, unless an appropriate risk assessment is undertaken to confirm that an adequate risk profile is achieved with a lower earthquake loading. Additionally, for the post-closure case, ANCOLD recommend that all tailings dams are evaluated for stability using probabilistic ground motion from a 1 in 10,000 AEP event. This is due to the long life expectation of tailings dams in the post-closure phase.\n\n**2019 Updates:**\nSome of the important matters covered in this revision include reinforcement of the need for robust management practices, updates on earthquake considerations to align with the ANCOLD Guidelines for Design of Dams and Appurtenant Structures for Earthquake (2019) and additional information on static liquefaction.\n\nThe search results indicate that the 2019 ANCOLD guidelines addendum specifically addresses seismic design criteria for tailings dams with reference to annual exceedance probabilities, though the full detailed technical specifications appear to be contained in the complete guideline documents referenced in the search results."],"durationSeconds":7.023863771999997}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFcoFPcN4xyGZphF9qkM8E","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Hashash 2020 site amplification model NGA-East reference rock 3000 m/s ground motion\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Data-driven adjustments for combined use of NGA-East hard-rock ground motion and site amplification models - María E Ramos-Sepúlveda, Jonathan P Stewart, Grace A Parker, Morgan P Moschetti, Eric M Thompson, Scott J Brandenberg, Youssef MA Hashash, Ellen M Rathje, 2024\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/87552930241231825\"},{\"title\":\"Ergodic Site Amplification Model for Central and Eastern North America | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/337624406_Ergodic_Site_Amplification_Model_for_Central_and_Eastern_North_America\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear site amplification model for ergodic seismic hazard analysis in Central and Eastern North America - Youssef M. A. Hashash, Okan Ilhan, Joseph A. Harmon, Grace A. Parker, Jonathan P. Stewart, Ellen M. Rathje, Kenneth W. Campbell, Walter J. Silva, 2020\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193\"},{\"title\":\"Simulated site amplification for Central and Eastern North America: Data set development and amplification models - Okan Ilhan, Youssef MA Hashash, Jonathan P Stewart, Ellen M Rathje, Sissy Nikolaou, Kenneth W Campbell, 2024\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/87552930231215723\"},{\"title\":\"Significance of site natural period effects for linear site amplification in central and eastern North America: Empirical and simulation-based models - Youssef M. A. Hashash, Okan Ilhan, Behzad Hassani, Gail M. Atkinson, Joseph Harmon, Hua Shao, 2020\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878198\"},{\"title\":\"NgaEast (nshmp-lib)\",\"url\":\"https://ghsc.code-pages.usgs.gov/nshmp/nshmp-lib/gov/usgs/earthquake/nshmp/gmm/NgaEast.html\"},{\"title\":\"Data-driven adjustments for combined use of NGA-East hard-rock ground motion and site amplification models | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/380370589_Data-driven_adjustments_for_combined_use_of_NGA-East_hard-rock_ground_motion_and_site_amplification_models\"},{\"title\":\"Data-driven adjustments for combined use of NGA-East hard-rock ground motion and site amplification models\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70252441\"},{\"title\":\"Performance of NGA-East GMMs and Site Amplification Models Relative to CENA Ground Motions\",\"url\":\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4580n511\"},{\"title\":\"Performance of NGA-East GMMs and Site Amplification ...\",\"url\":\"https://escholarship.org/content/qt4580n511/qt4580n511_noSplash_d82f0f1088e974da1b92bbeb1b31bac3.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found comprehensive information about the Hashash 2020 site amplification model for NGA-East with the reference rock condition you mentioned.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nThe Hashash et al. (2020) article presents recommendations for nonlinear site amplification models in Central and Eastern North America (CENA), with two median nonlinear amplification models produced as a function of VS30 and peak ground acceleration for reference conditions of VS = 3000 m/s and VS30 = 760 m/s.\n\nThe site amplification model predicts the natural log of the amplification (FS = ln(Amp)) and consists of three additive components that represent: (1) an F760 model that describes the linear-elastic site amplification of VS30 = 760 m/s relative to VS30 = 3000 m/s, (2) an FV model that describes the linear-elastic VS30 scaling relative to VS30 = 760 m/s, and (3) a nonlinear Fnl model that incorporates the effect of ground motion intensity.\n\nThe empirical models are derived from residuals analyses of ground-motion models for two reference conditions: B/C boundary (VS30 = 760 m/s) and CENA hard-rock condition (VS = 3000 m/s).\n\nHashash et al. (2020) also recommend the PGA-rock reference value (Vs30 = 3000 m/s) in the non-linear site amplification term not be allowed to exceed 1.0 g.\n\nOn its own, NGA-East is a hard rock model returning results for a site class where Vs30 = 3000 m/s.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"f4057e05-a3a5-4612-954d-779a7f5aea43","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:42:52.853Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Hashash 2020 site amplification model NGA-East reference rock 3000 m/s ground motion","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017n7U4sTTHRkGjCCiko8UT2","content":[{"title":"Data-driven adjustments for combined use of NGA-East hard-rock ground motion and site amplification models - María E Ramos-Sepúlveda, Jonathan P Stewart, Grace A Parker, Morgan P Moschetti, Eric M Thompson, Scott J Brandenberg, Youssef MA Hashash, Ellen M Rathje, 2024","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/87552930241231825"},{"title":"Ergodic Site Amplification Model for Central and Eastern North America | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/337624406_Ergodic_Site_Amplification_Model_for_Central_and_Eastern_North_America"},{"title":"Nonlinear site amplification model for ergodic seismic hazard analysis in Central and Eastern North America - Youssef M. A. Hashash, Okan Ilhan, Joseph A. Harmon, Grace A. Parker, Jonathan P. Stewart, Ellen M. Rathje, Kenneth W. Campbell, Walter J. Silva, 2020","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193"},{"title":"Simulated site amplification for Central and Eastern North America: Data set development and amplification models - Okan Ilhan, Youssef MA Hashash, Jonathan P Stewart, Ellen M Rathje, Sissy Nikolaou, Kenneth W Campbell, 2024","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/87552930231215723"},{"title":"Significance of site natural period effects for linear site amplification in central and eastern North America: Empirical and simulation-based models - Youssef M. A. Hashash, Okan Ilhan, Behzad Hassani, Gail M. Atkinson, Joseph Harmon, Hua Shao, 2020","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878198"},{"title":"NgaEast (nshmp-lib)","url":"https://ghsc.code-pages.usgs.gov/nshmp/nshmp-lib/gov/usgs/earthquake/nshmp/gmm/NgaEast.html"},{"title":"Data-driven adjustments for combined use of NGA-East hard-rock ground motion and site amplification models | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/380370589_Data-driven_adjustments_for_combined_use_of_NGA-East_hard-rock_ground_motion_and_site_amplification_models"},{"title":"Data-driven adjustments for combined use of NGA-East hard-rock ground motion and site amplification models","url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70252441"},{"title":"Performance of NGA-East GMMs and Site Amplification Models Relative to CENA Ground Motions","url":"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4580n511"},{"title":"Performance of NGA-East GMMs and Site Amplification ...","url":"https://escholarship.org/content/qt4580n511/qt4580n511_noSplash_d82f0f1088e974da1b92bbeb1b31bac3.pdf"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found comprehensive information about the Hashash 2020 site amplification model for NGA-East with the reference rock condition you mentioned.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nThe Hashash et al. (2020) article presents recommendations for nonlinear site amplification models in Central and Eastern North America (CENA), with two median nonlinear amplification models produced as a function of VS30 and peak ground acceleration for reference conditions of VS = 3000 m/s and VS30 = 760 m/s.\n\nThe site amplification model predicts the natural log of the amplification (FS = ln(Amp)) and consists of three additive components that represent: (1) an F760 model that describes the linear-elastic site amplification of VS30 = 760 m/s relative to VS30 = 3000 m/s, (2) an FV model that describes the linear-elastic VS30 scaling relative to VS30 = 760 m/s, and (3) a nonlinear Fnl model that incorporates the effect of ground motion intensity.\n\nThe empirical models are derived from residuals analyses of ground-motion models for two reference conditions: B/C boundary (VS30 = 760 m/s) and CENA hard-rock condition (VS = 3000 m/s).\n\nHashash et al. (2020) also recommend the PGA-rock reference value (Vs30 = 3000 m/s) in the non-linear site amplification term not be allowed to exceed 1.0 g.\n\nOn its own, NGA-East is a hard rock model returning results for a site class where Vs30 = 3000 m/s."],"durationSeconds":7.187076342999993}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01PDQU3384AahSjsUi8dEKc2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough information to write the chunk reviews. Let me now create the 6 chunk review files.\n\nLet me summarize the key findings for each chunk:\n\n**Chunk 001 (criteria_MCER.qmd)**:\n\nParagraph 1: ASCE/SEI 7-22 adopted by IBC 2021, §11.4.7 procedures for site-specific analysis.\n- Confirmed: ASCE 7-22 Chapter 11 and Chapter 21 are properly referenced.\n- The requirement to apply §21.1 and §21.2 when doing site-specific ground motions is standard ASCE 7 procedure.\n\n\n- Verified against the ASCE 7-22 standard documentation.\n\nParagraph 2: §11.4.7 indicates that §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied when performing site response analysis. The content is accurate, though there's a minor grammar issue with subject-verb agreement.\n\nParagraph 3: When §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design spectrum per §21.3 applies—I'm checking the continuation of this requirement.\n\nParagraph 4: MCER is defined as having a 1% probability of collapse in 50 years, with scale factors of 1.2, 1.25, and 1.3 for maximum horizontal components. I've confirmed the 1% probability definition and verified these scale factors are used in §21.1 for converting geometric mean to RotD100, though the upper period factor (1.3 for periods ≥ 10 seconds) differs slightly from older standards.\n\nParagraph 5: Moving into design-level spectral acceleration parameters...\n\nParagraph 6: The SDS formula uses a 0.9 factor applied to the maximum spectral acceleration across the 0.2 to 5-second period range, with the T* cutoff at 2 seconds determined by the site's Vs30 value of 442 m/s—both provisions from ASCE 7-22 §21.4 that were introduced in the 2016 edition.\n\nParagraph 7: The SMS and SM1 relationships are derived by multiplying SDS and SD1 by 1.5 respectively, though I'm noticing the knowledge base has a critical error where it defines SM1 = 1.5 SM1, which is circular—it should be SM1 = 1.5 SD1 instead. The SMS = 1.5 SDS relationship checks out as the inverse of the earlier SDS = (2/3)SMS formula.\n\nAlso, the document uses \"con\" instead of \"with\" in the T* formula, which suggests it was originally written in Spanish or translated from Spanish.\n\nMoving to the tailings storage facility criteria section, I'm seeing three major international frameworks referenced: GISTM 2020, CDA 2021, and ANCOLD 2019 guidelines, all of which I can verify through their respective published standards.\n\nThe design earthquake requirements shift depending on the lifecycle phase, with all three standards converging on a 1/10,000 annual exceedance probability or maximum credible earthquake during post-closure operations.\n\nLooking at the specific approaches: GISTM uses a single earthquake design ground motion with MCE as the upper limit, CDA employs a spectrum-compatible earthquake with MCE as the ceiling, and ANCOLD takes a dual-level approach with a uniform operational basis earthquake at 1/500 AEP. The ANCOLD 1/500 threshold aligns with the documentation, though CDA's minimum design criterion for high consequence dams—around 1/2,475 year return period—doesn't quite match the 1/10,000 figure referenced for high consequence classifications. During construction, the standards address operational basis and spectrum-compatible earthquake criteria with temporary safety factors.\n\nNow examining the operational phase: GISTM ranges from 1/200 to 1/10,000 AEP, CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000, and ANCOLD specifies 1/500 for the operational basis earthquake with spectrum-compatible earthquakes reaching 1/1,000 to 1/10,000. The ANCOLD 1/500 is confirmed, but the broader ranges for GISTM and CDA haven't been fully validated through the search results, and there's a reference to a 0.1% AEP storm pond criterion that needs clarification.\n\nFor post-closure, all three standards require specific AEP thresholds, with ANCOLD's 1/10,000 AEP requirement confirmed. The final section on rehabilitation measures presents general engineering principles that align with established practice.\n\nMoving into the disaggregation document now, looking at the PSHA hazard disaggregation methodology and the foundational Bazzurro & Cornell 1999 reference, which appears to be a key publication on this topic. I'm noticing some notation issues in the disaggregation probability equation—the brackets appear unmatched and the formulation seems off, though the normalization condition should hold. The modal scenario description and Bayes' theorem application are sound, and the marginal distributions for magnitude, distance, and epsilon are correctly derived through double summation, though there's some inconsistency in how the subscript indexing is presented.\n\nMoving into the hazard section, I'm checking the PSHA methodology references—Cornell 1968 and McGuire 2004 are indeed the standard foundational works for this framework. The hazard integral itself follows the conventional PSHA formulation.\n\nFor the probability framework, McGuire 2004 and Baker 2021 are both appropriate citations for explaining PDFs and the total probability theorem in the context of aleatory variability. The multi-source summation equation is mathematically sound.\n\nOn the GMPE integration, the document correctly identifies that the median prediction and total standard deviation are what feed into PSHA, with the upper tail of the log-normal distribution being particularly important at longer return periods.\n\nNow I'm verifying the Poisson process assumptions and the annual exceedance probability formula—the standard form P_1yr = 1 - exp[-λ(i*)] checks out. For the return period relationship, T_R = 1/λ_I(i*) is the correct definition, and I'm working through the numerical example where λ = 0.0021/year should give roughly 10% probability in 50 years.\n\nMoving into the site response section, I'm reviewing how near-surface geology modifies seismic waves through Vs30-based GMPE site terms versus external amplification models. The dual-mechanism approach is well-established in the literature, and I'm setting up a logic tree that spans Vs30 values from 200 to 1100 m/s to capture the range of site conditions.\n\nFor the amplification model itself, I'm using Stewart's formulation with a reference velocity of 760 m/s, which decomposes into three components—the linear site amplification, velocity-dependent effects, and nonlinear effects. This three-component structure aligns with how the lognormal amplification factor and its dispersion are being modeled.\n\nThe Hashash framework for CENA uses a different reference rock velocity of 3000 m/s, and the variance decomposition makes sense—the inelastic component only contributes when using that higher reference velocity, which is consistent with the NGA-East approach.\n\nFor the actual site amplification calculation, I'm applying the amplification factor to the reference ground motion to get the site-specific response. The envelope approach of taking the maximum between the GMPE and the amplification model provides a conservative bound for design purposes.\n\nNow I'm looking at how Vs30 is defined and its history—it's the harmonic average of shear wave velocities in the top 30 meters, a standard that emerged in the early 1990s. I'm verifying the NEHRP site classification system with its Vs30 velocity thresholds—Classes A through F are defined by specific boundaries, with Class A being the stiffest (>1500 m/s) down to Class E for soft soils (<180 m/s), plus Class F for special cases requiring evaluation.\n\nLooking at the Australian standards, AS 1726-2017 focuses on geotechnical investigation procedures rather than earthquake actions, while AS 1170.4 is the relevant seismic code but doesn't explicitly use Vs30 as a parameter. I'm also noting that the table uses Quarto syntax for inclusion.\n\nFor ASCE 7-22 and NBC-2020, both employ Vs30 (represented as $\\bar v_S$ in ASCE) for site classification, with the Canadian code also incorporating SPT values and undrained shear strength as alternative parameters.\n\nNow I'm moving through the document chunk by chunk to add confidence ratings and audit footnotes. I'll preserve the original content exactly while appending metadata about confidence levels and rationale for each paragraph, keeping mathematical equations and code blocks separate from these annotations. I've identified several issues to flag, including a self-referential equation in Chunk 001 where $S_{M1}=1.5 S_{M1}$ should read $S_{M1}=1.5 S_{D1}$, and a typo where \"con\" appears instead of \"with\" in a formula.\n\nFor Chunk 002, I need to verify the AEP ranges cited for GISTM operations and check whether the CDA mining dams bulletin reference is from 2014 or the more recent 2021 version. Chunk 003 has inconsistent subscript notation and a malformed equation with unmatched brackets that needs correction. Chunks 004, 005, and 006 appear generally sound with proper citations, though I should continue reviewing the remaining sections.\n\nNow I'm checking the Borcherdt 2012 citation to ensure it's properly formatted and accurately referenced. The document continues with the mathematical formulation for design-level spectral accelerations, showing how they're derived by scaling the MCER ordinates by a factor of 2/3, then presents the site-specific procedure formulas for calculating SDS and SD1 parameters, followed by the relationships for spectral response accelerations at short periods. I'm continuing through the document structure, tracking the display math blocks for the spectral response acceleration parameters. I need to audit paragraphs 5 through 7, making sure to place footnotes appropriately after the equations that follow introductory text.\n\nFor paragraph 5, the prose ends with a colon leading directly into the equation block, so the footnote should go after the text but before the display math. I'm working through the placement rules to ensure consistency across all paragraphs with equations. I see the pattern now—when a paragraph ends with prose that introduces a display math block, the audit footnote goes right after the prose text, before the math. So for P5, the footnote follows the colon at the end of \"[@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]:\" and then the equation displays below. Same structure applies to P6 and P7, where the prose introduction gets the footnote, and the math blocks follow as non-prose content. I'm looking at the structure of the document more carefully now. The formulas for SDS and SD1 come before paragraph 7, which introduces the SMS and SM1 calculations. I need to add footnotes after each paragraph to cite the standards they reference, particularly noting that the 0.9 factors and the Vs30 threshold align with ASCE 7-22 provisions.\n\nFor paragraph 7, there's a language inconsistency where \"con\" appears instead of \"with,\" suggesting this may have been translated from Spanish. More importantly, the display math that follows has a critical error—the equation for SM1 is self-referential, defining SM1 in terms of itself rather than SM1. I should place the audit footnote after the paragraph text, then flag the mathematical error in the display block that follows. so I should place a footnote after the display math block. Now I'm setting up the output structure with the session directory path and identifying the chunk files I need to generate.\n\nStarting with chunk-001, which covers the MCER and elastic design spectra section from ASCE 7-22, explaining how seismic demand was derived using site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum data and the allowances for replacing mapped spectral accelerations with probabilistic hazard analysis results.\n\nNow looking at the requirements from ASCE 7 sections 11.4.7 through 21.4—when performing site response and ground motion hazard analyses, the design response spectrum must follow section 21.3 procedures, acceleration parameters must comply with section 21.4, and the probabilistic spectral accelerations should represent the 5% damped response spectrum with a 1% probability of collapse over 50 years, with scaling applied if the ground motion models don't capture maximum horizontal response.\n\nThe scaling factors vary by period—1.2 for periods up to 0.2 seconds, 1.25 at 1.0 second, and 1.3 for periods of 10 seconds or greater—and the design-level spectral accelerations are then obtained by scaling the MCER values by a factor of 2/3 to get the design acceleration parameters.\n\nFor site-specific procedures, I'm calculating the short-period design acceleration as 0.9 times the maximum spectral acceleration across the 0.2 to 5 second range, and the long-period design acceleration as the greater of 0.9 times the maximum of period-weighted spectral acceleration (with the upper period limit depending on average shear wave velocity) or the spectral acceleration at 1 second.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the mapped spectral accelerations convert to design-level values—multiplying both the short-period and 1-second parameters by 1.5 to get the maximum considered earthquake spectral accelerations. \") is standard practice, and the formulas for SDS and SD1 align with ASCE 7-22. For P7, the SMS and SM1 formulas are also correctly stated per the standard. Overall, the technical content is accurate with high to medium-high confidence across all paragraphs, though there's a minor grammar correction needed in P2.\n\nWait, I'm catching an issue with the SM1 equation—it appears to be self-referential as written, when it should actually reference SD1 instead.\n\nLet me reconsider the document structure here. The prose in P7 ends with a colon, which means the audit footnote should come after the display math block where the actual error occurs, not before it. I'm working through the ASCE seismic design specifications, parsing out the key sections on MCER and elastic design spectra. The content includes several paragraphs of explanatory text interspersed with mathematical formulas defining spectral response accelerations and their relationships, particularly the design-level and site-specific procedures for calculating SDS and SD1 values. I'm working through the seismic design requirements from ASCE 7-22 as adopted in IBC 2021, noting that the standard allows substituting mapped spectral accelerations with probabilistic hazard analysis results when site-response modeling follows the specified procedures. with ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21. Once either §21.1 or §21.2 procedures are applied, the design response spectrum follows §21.3, and the design acceleration parameters are determined via §21.4—this procedural chain is directly supported by the standard and represents high confidence in the technical accuracy.\n\nNow I'm looking at the definition of probabilistic spectral response accelerations, which represent the maximum horizontal response using a 5% damped spectrum with a 1% collapse probability over 50 years. When ground motion models don't directly capture the maximum horizontal response, scaling factors are applied—1.2 for periods ≤0.2s, 1.25 at 1.0s, and 1.3 for periods ≥10s—assuming the models predict geometric mean or similar metrics of the two horizontal components. of MCER as a 5% damped spectrum targeting a 1% probability of collapse in 50 years is consistent with the risk-targeted ground motion framework codified in ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22. The scale factors for converting geometric mean ground-motion predictions to the maximum horizontal component are specified in ASCE 7-22 §21.1: 1.2 for T ≤ 0.2 s, 1.25 at T = 1.0 s, and 1.3 for T ≥ 10 s, with linear interpolation in log-log space for intermediate periods. These values align with the RotD100/RotD50 ratio research underlying the standard, though I'm noting medium confidence here since the original text doesn't explicitly detail the interpolation method for intermediate periods. To get the design-level spectral accelerations, I apply the standard two-thirds scaling factor to the MCER ordinates per ASCE 7-22 §21.3, which is a well-established relationship with high confidence.\n\nFor site-specific procedures, the design acceleration parameters use 0.9-scaled envelope values over prescribed period ranges, representing a significant shift from earlier editions that relied on simpler two-period parameters.  The threshold velocity of 442 m/s marking the Site Class C/D boundary and the two-second versus five-second upper-period limits align with ASCE 7-22 §21.4, though I notice the equation contains a Spanish-language label \"con\" that should be replaced with the English equivalent \"with\" or \"where\" to maintain consistency with the document's language requirements.\n\nNow I'm looking at the formulas for the spectral response acceleration parameters—the equations define $S_{DS}$ as the maximum spectral acceleration over a two-second period range and $S_{D1}$ as the maximum of either 0.9 times the spectral acceleration-period product over a specified range or the one-second spectral acceleration, with the threshold period $T^*$ switching between 2 and 5 seconds depending on whether the shear wave velocity exceeds 442 m/s. The next step involves calculating the short-period and one-second spectral response acceleration parameters. I see the critical error now—the equation shows $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$, which is just a tautology that doesn't make sense. The correct relationship should be $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$, following the same (2/3)-factor pattern from ASCE 7-22. This is a transcription mistake that needs fixing to avoid confusing readers about the proper conversion between design-level and MCER-level parameters.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the document structure—section headings like the MCER and Elastic Design Spectra heading shouldn't get audit footnotes, only the actual content paragraphs should. I need to be careful about preserving the original structure exactly and only appending footnotes where they belong, making sure the Pandoc syntax places them right after the text they annotate. I'm working through where to place the confidence footnotes in each paragraph—they need to go at the end of the prose text, after citations but before any display math that follows. Now I'm ready to write out all six file chunks, making sure to preserve the original formatting including blank lines and YAML frontmatter where they exist. I'm seeing that hazard disaggregation breaks down PSHA results into bins defined by magnitude, distance, and ground-motion residual to reveal which scenario combinations drive the hazard at a given threshold. The analysis is performed at a specific hazard level and produces conditional probability distributions that identify the controlling scenarios. The next section walks through the mathematical formulation of disaggregation, starting with the joint exceedance rate for specific scenario parameters and then breaking down how we partition the parameter space into bins for magnitude, distance, and residuals. After presenting those rate equations, it moves into conditional probabilities—calculating the likelihood that a particular bin is responsible for exceeding a ground-motion intensity level, along with marginal distributions across individual parameters. The final part emphasizes how disaggregation provides an empirical foundation for understanding which earthquake scenarios drive seismic hazard at a given site. I'm checking the structure of paragraph 3 — it ends with a colon leading into the marginal distribution equations, so the audit footnote should come after that colon. Then paragraph 4 follows the display math blocks.\n\nLooking at the disaggregation probability formula, there's an inconsistency in the subscript notation: the denominator uses $\\lambda_{I}(i^{*})$ while the numerator uses $\\lambda_{k,j\\ell}(i^{*})$, and the intermediate term $\\theta_{j,k,l}$ has the indices in a different order than the left side of the equation. I'm noticing there are several notation inconsistencies throughout the equations. The subscript ordering for theta is inconsistent—it's defined as (k,j,ℓ) but appears as (j,k,l) in the marginal equations, which breaks the pattern. There's also a malformed normalization condition with unmatched brackets that should be cleaned up to properly express the probability constraint.\n\nNow I'm ready to compile the actual review notes for each chunk, starting with the issues I've identified so far.\n\nI notice the Kramer reference is cited in chunk-003, not chunk-002 as I initially thought. Looking at the disaggregation section, the citation appears to reference Kramer's geotechnical earthquake engineering work, though there's a potential year discrepancy between the citation (1997) and the actual publication date (1996) that I should flag.\n\nI'm also noticing that chunk-004 references the OpenQuake Engine without a publication year since it's software, and chunk-005 contains cross-references to equations in the hazard chapter that I need to track.\n\nNow I'm working through the chunk files systematically. For chunk-003, I need to be careful about where the audit footnote goes—specifically around line 32 where there's text ending with a colon before display math equations, so the footnote should come before those math blocks rather than after.\n\nLooking at chunk-005 more carefully, I'm parsing through the YAML frontmatter and then identifying where the actual paragraphs begin so I can structure the footnotes correctly. I'm re-reading through the site effects section to make sure I have the structure right—it looks like there are multiple paragraphs describing how site effects are modeled through GMPE-based and amplification-based approaches, with several equations defining the spectral ordinates and how they're combined across different return periods and site classes. The amplification factor follows a lognormal distribution where the log-mean