{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016WgKxniPUq9v8VQ6KxT4WV","type":"tool_result","content":"Todos have been modified successfully. Ensure that you continue to use the todo list to track your progress. Please proceed with the current tasks if applicable"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"664ac452-c419-4397-9601-c9ab10b4b68e","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:49:41.399Z","tool_use_result":{"oldTodos":[{"content":"Write chunk-001 review (criteria_MCER.qmd)","status":"in_progress","activeForm":"Writing chunk-001 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-002 review (criteria_TSF.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-002 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-003 review (disaggregation.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-003 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-004 review (hazard.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-004 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-005 review (site.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-005 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-006 review (site_class.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-006 review"},{"content":"Assemble final review manuscript from chunks","status":"pending","activeForm":"Assembling final review manuscript"}],"newTodos":[{"content":"Write chunk-001 review (criteria_MCER.qmd)","status":"completed","activeForm":"Writing chunk-001 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-002 review (criteria_TSF.qmd)","status":"in_progress","activeForm":"Writing chunk-002 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-003 review (disaggregation.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-003 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-004 review (hazard.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-004 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-005 review (site.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-005 review"},{"content":"Write chunk-006 review (site_class.qmd)","status":"pending","activeForm":"Writing chunk-006 review"},{"content":"Assemble final review manuscript from chunks","status":"pending","activeForm":"Assembling final review manuscript"}],"verificationNudgeNeeded":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01BjQHZLzquowShEDc1Vjg5x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BDWCAVJNDasTqJeRdShSvY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_214042/psha_hive2.chunk-002.review.md","content":"\n\nThree international frameworks govern the seismic design of tailings storage facilities (TSFs): the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management [@gistm2020], the Canadian Dam Association guidelines [@cda2021], and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams [@ancold2019]. Each framework defines performance objectives and design earthquake criteria on the basis of two factors: the consequence classification of the facility and its lifecycle phase. Consequence classification reflects the potential severity of a dam failure, assessed through population at risk (PAR), anticipated loss of life, and the magnitude of environmental and socio-economic impacts. All three standards define consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme, with progressively more stringent seismic criteria as the consequence class increases. The classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake annual exceedance probabilities (AEPs) for each standard are tabulated in Appendix [-@sec-criteria_appendix] (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], and [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and scope of all three referenced frameworks is confirmed. GISTM was published in August 2020 by UNEP, PRI, and ICMM and defines consequence classifications ranging from Low to Extreme with progressively stringent design criteria [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analysis (2021) is a published CDA guidance document [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021]. The ANCOLD 2019 revision to the Guidelines on Tailings Dams is a published guideline [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The characterisation of the consequence classification system and its role in determining seismic design criteria is consistent with all three sources. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nFor a given consequence class, the applicable design earthquake also varies by lifecycle phase. Each phase of a TSF's life — construction, operation, closure, and post-closure — presents distinct engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions presented in this chapter are organized into three stages corresponding to the principal design earthquake levels: operational (OBE), closure (SEE), and post-closure (PCE). The assignment of AEPs to each stage differs between standards and is most differentiated by consequence class during operations, where AEPs range from 1/100 to 1/10,000 depending on standard and consequence class (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], [-@tbl-ancold]). During post-closure, this differentiation collapses: all three frameworks require convergence to the most stringent criterion — the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) or the 1/10,000-year event — irrespective of the original consequence class. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general structure of lifecycle-phased design earthquake criteria in all three frameworks is consistent with published descriptions of GISTM [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf] and ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The statement that all three frameworks converge to MCE or 1/10,000-year AEP at post-closure is confirmed for ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific AEP range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 during operations across all three standards could not be verified in full from publicly available excerpts; the GISTM and CDA specific AEP tables are referenced in appendices rather than reproduced in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the post-closure convergence claim is supported but the specific operational AEP range requires direct reference to the appendix tables for complete verification.]\n\nThe approaches differ in structure. Under GISTM [-@gistm2020], a single earthquake design ground motion (EDGM) is specified for each consequence class and phase; the MCE must also be evaluated and governs if it produces higher demand than the probabilistic criterion. Importantly, GISTM applies the same AEPs during operations and active closure, with a step increase to a uniform 1/10,000 AEP for all consequence classes only at passive closure (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA [-@cda2021] employs the Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) as the design basis, with the MCE applied as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities; AEPs are progressively tightened from operations through closure, and risk-based approaches may be permitted for lower consequence classes during transitional closure phases, provided that post-closure requirements are ultimately satisfied (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD [-@ancold2019] applies a dual-level methodology, assigning a uniform Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE) of approximately 1/500 AEP across all consequence categories during operations, with a Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) that varies from 1/1,000 to 1/10,000 depending on consequence class; the most severe criteria (MCE or 1/10,000-year event, 85th percentile) are mandated for Extreme-class facilities and required for all dams prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD dual-level framework with an approximately 1/500 AEP OBE and a consequence-class-dependent SEE is confirmed by the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines addendum [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The ANCOLD requirement for Extreme-class and pre-abandonment facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific structural descriptions of GISTM and CDA (single EDGM vs. SEE-based approaches, passive closure step increase to 1/10,000 AEP for GISTM, risk-based transition provisions for CDA) are broadly consistent with publicly available summaries [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf][WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. However, specific AEP tables and thresholds for GISTM and CDA are not fully reproduced in the publicly accessible sources reviewed; verification relies on the referenced appendix tables. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n\n### Construction\n\nTailing deposits are frequently constructed in stages, with embankments raised incrementally. Seismic stability during construction is a critical consideration, as the dam may be at partial height or incomplete strength. Even with a low probability of major earthquake occurrence during the relatively brief construction phase, the potential consequences of a moderate event on an incomplete dam can be severe. Accordingly, design requirements stipulate that temporary factors of safety under seismic loading must be satisfied at all construction stages. Initial and intermediate dam stages are analyzed for at least OBE-level events; for facilities with high consequence classifications or in high-seismicity regions, SEE criteria may also be applied. Temporary material properties and geometries are used in analyses, with design iterations as the dam evolves. Standards (e.g., CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014) recommend explicit consideration of seismic hazards at every project phase, supported by independent review at critical milestones. Construction methods and schedules are adjusted as needed to maintain seismic safety, including limiting impounded water volumes or avoiding vulnerable states during periods of heightened seismic risk. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general principles described — phased construction analysis, OBE and SEE application during construction, use of temporary material properties, and independent review at critical milestones — are consistent with current dam engineering practice and with the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines for tailings dams [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The reference to a \"CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014\" cannot be verified against the CDA's current publication list from available sources; the CDA has published a series of technical bulletins on tailings dam topics, but the specific 2014 bulletin cited is not prominently confirmed in the public record for 2021 edition references [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. This citation warrants verification. The substantive engineering claims are reasonable and consistent with industry practice, but the unverified citation reduces confidence to MEDIUM.]\n\n### Operation (Active Life)\n\nDuring operations, when the facility contains large volumes of tailings and water, the risk profile is at its highest and the design earthquake criteria are most differentiated by consequence class. Under GISTM, operational AEPs range from 1/200 for Low-consequence facilities to 1/10,000 for Extreme (Table [-@tbl-gistm]); CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000 (Table [-@tbl-cda]); and ANCOLD assigns a uniform OBE at 1/500 AEP with an SEE that varies from 1/1,000 to MCE or 1/10,000 by consequence category (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). The dam must safely withstand the OBE without operational interruption and must not release tailings under the SEE. Water management is integral — adequate freeboard must be maintained to accommodate seismic settlement, wave run-up, or ground deformations. For high or extreme consequence dams, design includes provision for concurrent storm and seismic loading (e.g., maintaining a 0.1% AEP storm pond without overtopping during seismic events, in line with ANCOLD and international practice). Operational seismic readiness is maintained through regular surveillance (crack/deformation monitoring) and emergency response planning. Engineering features — such as buttresses, drainage to control pore pressures, and control of loose, saturated zones — can enhance seismic performance. If an OBE is exceeded, post-event inspection and prompt repairs are required to restore the safety margin. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD uniform OBE at approximately 1/500 AEP during operations and the consequence-dependent SEE range are confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific GISTM AEP range from 1/200 (Low) to 1/10,000 (Extreme) during operations and the CDA range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 are stated in reference to appendix tables and are plausible based on publicly available descriptions of these frameworks, but direct normative confirmation requires access to the full GISTM Annex 2 and CDA guidelines. The claim regarding concurrent storm and seismic loading at 0.1% AEP storm pond is consistent with ANCOLD practice but could not be independently confirmed from the publicly accessible ANCOLD 2019 addendum text. The general engineering principles (OBE without operational interruption, no tailings release under SEE, freeboard maintenance) are standard in dam engineering and consistent with known framework requirements. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverifiable specific AEP values and storm-seismic combination criterion.]\n\n### Closure and Post-Closure\n\nAfter the cessation of operations, TSFs are expected to remain physically stable for the indefinite future, in the absence of active management. Design for closure and post-closure is based on the principle that the probability of a rare seismic event approaches certainty over an infinite time horizon. The three frameworks handle the transition differently. Under GISTM, the same consequence-dependent AEPs that govern operations continue through active closure; only at passive closure does a uniform 1/10,000 AEP apply to all consequence classes (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA progressively tightens criteria: facilities classified as High or above must already meet 1/10,000 AEP at the closure stage, while lower classes transition from their operational levels toward more conservative values; all facilities must converge to 1/10,000 AEP or MCE before abandonment (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD mandates reassessment of all facilities under Extreme-class criteria (MCE or 1/10,000 AEP, 85th percentile) prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD requirement for all facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) prior to abandonment is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The GISTM structure — same AEPs during active closure, step to 1/10,000 at passive closure — and the CDA progressive tightening toward 1/10,000 or MCE before abandonment are consistent with publicly available descriptions [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. Full normative verification of the GISTM and CDA closure-phase AEP thresholds requires access to the complete framework documents. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n\nClosure design is typically more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities. Rehabilitation measures (flattened slopes, reinforcements, erosion protection) may be implemented to achieve maintenance-free performance. Permanent spillways are designed for probable maximum floods, eliminating reliance on operational water management. Post-closure stability analyses must explicitly account for the degradation of temporary features. Components not expected to persist (e.g., geomembranes) are excluded from the long-term stability evaluation; the residual embankment must independently resist seismic loading. Long-term factors of safety are typically higher, and materials (cemented tailings, stabilizing berms) may be specified to enhance seismic resilience. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general engineering principles described for post-closure design — exclusion of temporary components from long-term stability analysis, use of permanent spillways sized for probable maximum floods, and the requirement for higher long-term factors of safety — are consistent with established dam engineering practice and with the spirit of the ANCOLD 2019 and GISTM frameworks [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf][WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The claims are plausible and broadly supported, though no specific quantitative references are provided in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the paragraph presents general engineering principles without direct normative citations, making claim-level verification against specific standard requirements incomplete.]\n\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":351,"cache_read_input_tokens":93922,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":351,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"2d2de2ab-bca8-43d7-81ca-05e0f6c8f864"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BDWCAVJNDasTqJeRdShSvY","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_214042/psha_hive2.chunk-002.review.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5655a3d-d1a5-49f5-9bdd-236b5edf4893","uuid":"3ddad7ad-fff6-482c-af56-9f4362de9a08","timestamp":"2026-03-30T00:51:03.017Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_214042/psha_hive2.chunk-002.review.md","content":"\n\nThree international frameworks govern the seismic design of tailings storage facilities (TSFs): the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management [@gistm2020], the Canadian Dam Association guidelines [@cda2021], and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams [@ancold2019]. Each framework defines performance objectives and design earthquake criteria on the basis of two factors: the consequence classification of the facility and its lifecycle phase. Consequence classification reflects the potential severity of a dam failure, assessed through population at risk (PAR), anticipated loss of life, and the magnitude of environmental and socio-economic impacts. All three standards define consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme, with progressively more stringent seismic criteria as the consequence class increases. The classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake annual exceedance probabilities (AEPs) for each standard are tabulated in Appendix [-@sec-criteria_appendix] (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], and [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and scope of all three referenced frameworks is confirmed. GISTM was published in August 2020 by UNEP, PRI, and ICMM and defines consequence classifications ranging from Low to Extreme with progressively stringent design criteria [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analysis (2021) is a published CDA guidance document [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021]. The ANCOLD 2019 revision to the Guidelines on Tailings Dams is a published guideline [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The characterisation of the consequence classification system and its role in determining seismic design criteria is consistent with all three sources. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nFor a given consequence class, the applicable design earthquake also varies by lifecycle phase. Each phase of a TSF's life — construction, operation, closure, and post-closure — presents distinct engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions presented in this chapter are organized into three stages corresponding to the principal design earthquake levels: operational (OBE), closure (SEE), and post-closure (PCE). The assignment of AEPs to each stage differs between standards and is most differentiated by consequence class during operations, where AEPs range from 1/100 to 1/10,000 depending on standard and consequence class (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], [-@tbl-ancold]). During post-closure, this differentiation collapses: all three frameworks require convergence to the most stringent criterion — the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) or the 1/10,000-year event — irrespective of the original consequence class. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general structure of lifecycle-phased design earthquake criteria in all three frameworks is consistent with published descriptions of GISTM [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf] and ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The statement that all three frameworks converge to MCE or 1/10,000-year AEP at post-closure is confirmed for ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific AEP range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 during operations across all three standards could not be verified in full from publicly available excerpts; the GISTM and CDA specific AEP tables are referenced in appendices rather than reproduced in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the post-closure convergence claim is supported but the specific operational AEP range requires direct reference to the appendix tables for complete verification.]\n\nThe approaches differ in structure. Under GISTM [-@gistm2020], a single earthquake design ground motion (EDGM) is specified for each consequence class and phase; the MCE must also be evaluated and governs if it produces higher demand than the probabilistic criterion. Importantly, GISTM applies the same AEPs during operations and active closure, with a step increase to a uniform 1/10,000 AEP for all consequence classes only at passive closure (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA [-@cda2021] employs the Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) as the design basis, with the MCE applied as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities; AEPs are progressively tightened from operations through closure, and risk-based approaches may be permitted for lower consequence classes during transitional closure phases, provided that post-closure requirements are ultimately satisfied (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD [-@ancold2019] applies a dual-level methodology, assigning a uniform Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE) of approximately 1/500 AEP across all consequence categories during operations, with a Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) that varies from 1/1,000 to 1/10,000 depending on consequence class; the most severe criteria (MCE or 1/10,000-year event, 85th percentile) are mandated for Extreme-class facilities and required for all dams prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD dual-level framework with an approximately 1/500 AEP OBE and a consequence-class-dependent SEE is confirmed by the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines addendum [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The ANCOLD requirement for Extreme-class and pre-abandonment facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific structural descriptions of GISTM and CDA (single EDGM vs. SEE-based approaches, passive closure step increase to 1/10,000 AEP for GISTM, risk-based transition provisions for CDA) are broadly consistent with publicly available summaries [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf][WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. However, specific AEP tables and thresholds for GISTM and CDA are not fully reproduced in the publicly accessible sources reviewed; verification relies on the referenced appendix tables. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n\n### Construction\n\nTailing deposits are frequently constructed in stages, with embankments raised incrementally. Seismic stability during construction is a critical consideration, as the dam may be at partial height or incomplete strength. Even with a low probability of major earthquake occurrence during the relatively brief construction phase, the potential consequences of a moderate event on an incomplete dam can be severe. Accordingly, design requirements stipulate that temporary factors of safety under seismic loading must be satisfied at all construction stages. Initial and intermediate dam stages are analyzed for at least OBE-level events; for facilities with high consequence classifications or in high-seismicity regions, SEE criteria may also be applied. Temporary material properties and geometries are used in analyses, with design iterations as the dam evolves. Standards (e.g., CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014) recommend explicit consideration of seismic hazards at every project phase, supported by independent review at critical milestones. Construction methods and schedules are adjusted as needed to maintain seismic safety, including limiting impounded water volumes or avoiding vulnerable states during periods of heightened seismic risk. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general principles described — phased construction analysis, OBE and SEE application during construction, use of temporary material properties, and independent review at critical milestones — are consistent with current dam engineering practice and with the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines for tailings dams [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The reference to a \"CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014\" cannot be verified against the CDA's current publication list from available sources; the CDA has published a series of technical bulletins on tailings dam topics, but the specific 2014 bulletin cited is not prominently confirmed in the public record for 2021 edition references [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. This citation warrants verification. The substantive engineering claims are reasonable and consistent with industry practice, but the unverified citation reduces confidence to MEDIUM.]\n\n### Operation (Active Life)\n\nDuring operations, when the facility contains large volumes of tailings and water, the risk profile is at its highest and the design earthquake criteria are most differentiated by consequence class. Under GISTM, operational AEPs range from 1/200 for Low-consequence facilities to 1/10,000 for Extreme (Table [-@tbl-gistm]); CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000 (Table [-@tbl-cda]); and ANCOLD assigns a uniform OBE at 1/500 AEP with an SEE that varies from 1/1,000 to MCE or 1/10,000 by consequence category (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). The dam must safely withstand the OBE without operational interruption and must not release tailings under the SEE. Water management is integral — adequate freeboard must be maintained to accommodate seismic settlement, wave run-up, or ground deformations. For high or extreme consequence dams, design includes provision for concurrent storm and seismic loading (e.g., maintaining a 0.1% AEP storm pond without overtopping during seismic events, in line with ANCOLD and international practice). Operational seismic readiness is maintained through regular surveillance (crack/deformation monitoring) and emergency response planning. Engineering features — such as buttresses, drainage to control pore pressures, and control of loose, saturated zones — can enhance seismic performance. If an OBE is exceeded, post-event inspection and prompt repairs are required to restore the safety margin. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD uniform OBE at approximately 1/500 AEP during operations and the consequence-dependent SEE range are confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific GISTM AEP range from 1/200 (Low) to 1/10,000 (Extreme) during operations and the CDA range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 are stated in reference to appendix tables and are plausible based on publicly available descriptions of these frameworks, but direct normative confirmation requires access to the full GISTM Annex 2 and CDA guidelines. The claim regarding concurrent storm and seismic loading at 0.1% AEP storm pond is consistent with ANCOLD practice but could not be independently confirmed from the publicly accessible ANCOLD 2019 addendum text. The general engineering principles (OBE without operational interruption, no tailings release under SEE, freeboard maintenance) are standard in dam engineering and consistent with known framework requirements. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverifiable specific AEP values and storm-seismic combination criterion.]\n\n### Closure and Post-Closure\n\nAfter the cessation of operations, TSFs are expected to remain physically stable for the indefinite future, in the absence of active management. Design for closure and post-closure is based on the principle that the probability of a rare seismic event approaches certainty over an infinite time horizon. The three frameworks handle the transition differently. Under GISTM, the same consequence-dependent AEPs that govern operations continue through active closure; only at passive closure does a uniform 1/10,000 AEP apply to all consequence classes (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA progressively tightens criteria: facilities classified as High or above must already meet 1/10,000 AEP at the closure stage, while lower classes transition from their operational levels toward more conservative values; all facilities must converge to 1/10,000 AEP or MCE before abandonment (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD mandates reassessment of all facilities under Extreme-class criteria (MCE or 1/10,000 AEP, 85th percentile) prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD requirement for all facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) prior to abandonment is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The GISTM structure — same AEPs during active closure, step to 1/10,000 at passive closure — and the CDA progressive tightening toward 1/10,000 or MCE before abandonment are consistent with publicly available descriptions [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. Full normative verification of the GISTM and CDA closure-phase AEP thresholds requires access to the complete framework documents. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n\nClosure design is typically more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities. Rehabilitation measures (flattened slopes, reinforcements, erosion protection) may be implemented to achieve maintenance-free performance. Permanent spillways are designed for probable maximum floods, eliminating reliance on operational water management. Post-closure stability analyses must explicitly account for the degradation of temporary features. Components not expected to persist (e.g., geomembranes) are excluded from the long-term stability evaluation; the residual embankment must independently resist seismic loading. Long-term factors of safety are typically higher, and materials (cemented tailings, stabilizing berms) may be specified to enhance seismic resilience. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general engineering principles described for post-closure design — exclusion of temporary components from long-term stability analysis, use of permanent spillways sized for probable maximum floods, and the requirement for higher long-term factors of safety — are consistent with established dam engineering practice and with the spirit of the ANCOLD 2019 and GISTM frameworks [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf][WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The claims are plausible and broadly supported, though no specific quantitative references are provided in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the paragraph presents general engineering principles without direct normative citations, making claim-level verification against specific standard requirements incomplete.]\n\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
